{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/528642-m20-technocracy-human-condition-vs-demonic-influence","id":528642,"title":"M20 Technocracy: human condition vs demonic influence","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2015-05-26T01:49:27-07:00","id":528642,"title":"M20 Technocracy: human condition vs demonic influence","content":"This is a fork of the discussion about which book to use as source in a Technocratic campaign ( <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/517506-which-book-for-technocratic-game/\">http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/m...nocratic-game/</a> )<br /> <br /> The new topic is about the M20 statement that Technocracy is infested with Nephandi, even in its Inner Circle. This statement is, as many other things, presented as optional, but seems to be the default option (two of the three options assume that Nephandi are in charge). This concept blames the Nephandi and their masters of the corruption of Technocracy's original purpose.<br /> <br /> For me, what this idea suppose, is a devaluation of the Technocratic themes and moral issues. It blames otherwordly evil instead of human condition for the flaws. And it removes most of the moral dilemmas (a Manager who do wrong things to achieve right ends is disputable, a Progenitor who experiments with kids to find a cure is disputable, a barabbi who do those things out of pure evil is not).<br /> <hr /><br /> I continue the discussion with Heavy Arms where I left it:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/517506-which-book-for-technocratic-game?p=526931#post526931\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Can you actually pay attention to what I said instead of arguing stupid strawmen if we're actually going to discuss this?</div> </div> </div> </div>Please, be cool. I am not purposely throwing strawmen in order to discredit you. I may happen not to understand you and misinterpret your points. It also happens that English is not my native tongue, so sometimes I think to understand something different of what is really said.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/517506-which-book-for-technocratic-game?p=526931#post526931\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I said since the Technocracy victory, aka the Revised metaplot events.</div> </div> </div> </div>In this case, I thought that with the Technocracy victory you were referring of how world changed due to Technocracy dominion, not just when MRev declared Technocracy victory.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/517506-which-book-for-technocratic-game?p=526931#post526931\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Global warming is getting worse, and there continues to be minimal effort to counter it even as it has stopped being a theoretical dangerous and millions of people have had their lives negatively impacted by it. We have entire coastal communities being displaced as minimum sea level rises puts them underwater, with no where for them to go.<br /> <br /> [and goes on]</div> </div> </div> </div>Ok, here is my point. All those are terrible things. But it is simplistic to rely in demonic influence to explain every bad thing in our world.<br /> <br /> In real life those forces of evil do no exist, and we still have global warming, economic crisis, wars, human exploitation, etc, etc, etc. All these things are explained by flaws in human condition, but not flaws that make them serve devils, flaws that make that things happens by themselves. This include human ambition and cumulation of power.<br /> <br /> I know Technocracy do not exist as well, but I think it is a close metaphor for the real powers. Even if you are not interested in the metaphor, removing the Nephandic corruption as the motivation, we have to look for more complex causes, and we have a Technocracy much more interested and centered in human flaws (which is appropriate since Technocracy aims to Empower the humanity, but they fall by very human flaws).<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/517506-which-book-for-technocratic-game?p=526931#post526931\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">That's not what anyone is doing. Again, stop with the strawmen. The Nephandi are a symptom, not a cause. They see these things happening and they step in to make it worse. The Nephandi aren't to blame for the flaws of the Traditions and the Technocracy. They're there to make them worse.</div> </div> </div> </div>Ok, maybe we are not discussing the same exact point. I will make me clear: I don't mind if some Technocrats are corrupted by Nephandi, it even can be a good paranoid mood. What I don't like is that the Inner Circle is at the service of the Nephandi or that the Technocracy wrong doings are their work. In the &quot;Future Fates&quot; box, two of the three options blame the Nephandi, I'll add emphasis to the exact phrases:<br /> <br /> <i>\u2022 The Technocracy has Fallen: In this option, <b>the Inner Circle is essentially a Nephandic playground. Most ranking<br /> Technocrats are either barabbi or were never true Technocrats to begin with.</b> The Union has been exterminating its<br /> rivals, purging dissent, and driving humanity toward self-destruction. Although some Technocrats mean well and have<br /> been fighting against corruption, it\u2019s essentially a doomed effort. The Technocracy is fucked, and it has been for ages.<br /> \u2022 The Union has problems but is not metaphysically corrupt: Despite a few bad apples and a ruthless approach<br /> to Ascension, the Technocratic Union is not overrun with Nephandi. The corruption that does exist is an extension of<br /> hubris, not infiltration by the Fallen. Essentially, the Union means well but has a brutal way of taking care of business.<br /> \u2022 There\u2019s corruption, but they\u2019re dealing with it: Yes, <b>some Nephandi have warped the Union\u2019s purpose</b>. And<br /> yeah, the Technocracy\u2019s brutal. But still, the Union can be saved. Devoted Technocrats \u2013 inspired and sometimes led<br /> by Secret Agent John Courage \u2013 have been fighting a covert war within the Technocracy... and despite major losses<br /> and obstacles, there\u2019s still hope for the Technocracy\u2019s redemption.</i><br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/517506-which-book-for-technocratic-game?p=526931#post526931\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Likewise, Nephandi influence pushing the Tradition and Technocratic buttons to try to get them to indulge in their flaws doesn't down tone those flaws. It makes those flaws worse because it isn't just petty wastes of time and potential while good could have been done</div> </div> </div> </div>I understand your theory supposes that their flaws are there, but they would make no more harm than waste of potential. This is downtoning for me, because the good tone is where they arrive only by their flaws, only by themselves.<br /> <br /> Following the mafia analogy, you are admitting flawed Technocrats that can be bribed into doing bad things, but then, if they do that things for the bribe, the ultimate cause of it is pure evil and destroying the world. My point is that the Technocracy created the problem they alone.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/517506-which-book-for-technocratic-game?p=526931#post526931\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Otherwise it isn't a deal, it's demonic mind control.</div> </div> </div> </div>To be honest, I don't see why a Nephandus would not use mind control.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/517506-which-book-for-technocratic-game?p=526931#post526931\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The Nephandi are universally bad guys, sure, but plenty of them have complex motivations. Descent is not cartoonish evil. It's a very human evil.</div> </div> </div> </div>Is it? In real life there humans do not want to destroy the world and they don't want to do evil things just to make a worse world. Some crazy ass can exist, but very very few and he is probably under treatment and totally harmless.<br /> <br /> Human evil people do bad things in order to take benefit from them, like money or whatever other thing they are appealed to. Occasionally, some do evil things out of what they think it is a good cause (which is not destroying the world but defending their religion, freeing their people, achieving a more fair world, etc).<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/517506-which-book-for-technocratic-game?p=526931#post526931\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Besides losing a bit at the end there, I didn't say anything about the Nephandi not taking responsibility... so I have no idea what you're talking about.</div> </div> </div> </div>I wanted to say that the Nephandi do not remove the responsibility from the Technocrats.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/517506-which-book-for-technocratic-game?p=526931#post526931\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">However discussing this means actually talking to each other. It means not resorting to strawmen and hyperbole to make all your points. It means arguing in good faith. It means not forcing your personal opinions (you clearly don't like the Nephandi and have a strong personal view of them) as facts.</div> </div> </div> </div>I am arguing with good faith and I am not forcing nothing. I don't specially hate the Nephandi and I just don't mind them too much until they become the explanation of the Technocracy flaws.<br /> <br /> It's true though that I don't specially like them. Maybe I am wrong, but those Nephandic motivations are normally just excuses that do not make sense when you think carefully about them. So the world is inevitably ending, why accelerate it? And why is that you enjoy the process. You have said a lot of times that they are not cartoonishly evil, but that all that I read. At the end, they are evil evils of evilness dressed in some artificial excuse for a philosophy, and since that can have some appeal in some chronicles, I think that putting them pulling the strings of all the flaws of the Technocracy and the Council is a huge devaluation of the human theme.<br /> <br /> Aside from my personal tastes on Nephandi, the thruth is the truth, be it said by Agamemnon, or by his swineherd.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3673-mu_","name":"Mu_","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3673_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"99"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-26T03:27:08-07:00","id":528677,"title":"","content":"Across the book there is a lot of bad stuff did by the Order of Reason or the Technocracy (for example in the History section) that is just presented as caused due to its own failings.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1830-history","name":"History","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1830_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"158"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-26T04:08:53-07:00","id":528703,"title":"","content":"Power corrupts, absolute power well you know. I don't think the nephandi have been behind the technocracy but they are a handy lever to use for their plans.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3953-dwight","name":"Dwight","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3953_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2355"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-26T04:24:56-07:00","id":528708,"title":"","content":"I think that people are missing the point. The Nephandi don't need control, they just need to corrupt and deceive.<br /> A whisper here and there, the right suggestion at the right time, a compliment to the proud etc...They are the demon on your shoulder convincing you to be selfish because you deserve it, and worst of all they convince you that it was your idea. Bit by bit they feed the worst parts of your character till you become as bad as they are, and you will never suspect a thing.<br /> As for all these rumors and conspiracies of the nephandi ruling over the technocracy and the other factions? they are a lie but that's not the point, the point is that people believe it. The few nephandi infiltrated inside each faction fuel that lie and then sit back and laugh as the the mages tear each other apart and walk towards damnation. It's all a twisted spectacle of paranoia, backstabbing and distrust and the nephandi are having the time of their lives with it.<br /> At least this is the atmosphere that I believe that Satyros was trying to create in the game. It makes for a more interesting and terrifying game. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2964-firanai","name":"Firanai","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2964_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"672"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-26T07:03:39-07:00","id":528763,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Mu_</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/528642-m20-technocracy-human-condition-vs-demonic-influence#post528642\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">All those are terrible things. But it is simplistic to rely in demonic influence to explain every bad thing in our world.<br /> <br /> In real life those forces of evil do no exist...</div> </div> </div> </div>I agree that it's simplistic to rely on demonic influence to explain every bad thing in the world, and I'm not trying to derail your thread or anything, but your religious beliefs are not universal.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/173-morimaruvik","name":"Morimaruvik","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar173_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"126"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-26T08:04:49-07:00","id":528783,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Morimaruvik</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/528642-m20-technocracy-human-condition-vs-demonic-influence?p=528763#post528763\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I agree that it's simplistic to rely on demonic influence to explain every bad thing in the world, and I'm not trying to derail your thread or anything, but your religious beliefs are not universal.</div> </div> </div> </div>In fact, I don't have religious beliefs. Back when I was a Catholic, though, and that means 6 to 14 years old, I never thought that there were evil people walking around just doing evil for the sake of evil. I think very few people is so nav\u00efve to think that war, hunger or pollution are orchestated by some weird satanic cultists.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3673-mu_","name":"Mu_","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3673_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"99"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-26T08:17:33-07:00","id":528791,"title":"","content":"The belief that people are wrong regarding a religious tenet is religious, by definition, in English, as the primary definition of &quot;religious&quot; is &quot;of or pertaining to religion.&quot; So your belief that (for example) Christianity is wrong regarding the influence of demons (a belief of yours I tend to share) is, again by definition, a religious belief. So yeah, you have religious beliefs.<br /> <br /> Dumbing things down to &quot;war, hunger or pollution are orchestrated by some weird satanic cultists&quot; demonstrates that you never really understood the religious beliefs you now religiously disavow, and thus, indicate the flaw in the reasoning that generated the premise of this thread. In general Christian belief, it is neither demons or demonolators that instigate strife on earth, but rather the weaknesses of character that spawn such abuses that open people to demonic influence. The extrapolation of this theory regarding Nephandic influence within the Technocracy seems overly-obvious, so I won't belabor it.<br /> <br /> But again, I'm not trying to derail your thread. I just couldn't let your religious beliefs stated as fact go unchallenged. It would be like accepting the premise of &quot;Mage is dumb because the Celestial Chorus believes in God* and we all <i>know</i> God* doesn't exist so Mage is dumb.&quot; So carry on.<br /> <br /> * &quot;God&quot; being Yahweh, but that's another argument","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/173-morimaruvik","name":"Morimaruvik","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar173_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"126"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-26T08:54:03-07:00","id":528800,"title":"","content":"I don't want either to derail the conversation, but I feel I must make myself clear in that. I do not have religios beliefs nor I follow relious tenets. What I think, I think out of my experience and my logic, not to follow nor to reject some religion. If my views coincide with some religion (charity, all men are equals) or diverge (ethernal life, predestination, the devil whispering) is just coincidence, i does not turn them into religious beliefs.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3673-mu_","name":"Mu_","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3673_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"99"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-26T09:03:26-07:00","id":528805,"title":"","content":"A &quot;belief&quot; is &quot;the feeling that something is true.&quot; &quot;Religious&quot; is &quot;of or pertaining to religion.&quot; The idea that demons do or do not influence people is a religious belief.<br /> <br /> I don't want to belabor this and derail your thread, honestly, but all you're saying is &quot;the religious belief I stated in my initial post isn't really religious because I'm not religious, despite the fact I opened the thread with my religious beliefs that I say aren't religious because I don't consider myself religious.&quot;<br /> <br /> Demons are a religious concept, not scientific. Any belief regarding them is a religious belief, by definition. A lack of belief one way or the other...a lack of religious belief, in other words...would be more akin to &quot;no fucking clue, I've no experience with what those people are talking about.&quot; Even &quot;I lack experience with demons, therefore I have no reason to believe they exist.&quot;<br /> <br /> But stating as a fact that a religion is wrong about something is, again by definition in English, a religious belief.<br /> <br /> But I feel like I'm repeating myself, and I never intended to derail your thread, so I have nothing more to say on the matter barring further developments.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/173-morimaruvik","name":"Morimaruvik","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar173_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"126"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-26T09:12:37-07:00","id":528809,"title":"","content":"Brief reminder: perhaps the scariest thing about the Nephandi is that they <i>are</i> human. Twisted humans, yes. Humans corrupted by inhuman power, sure. Awakened humans dedicated to evil in ways most of us can't imagine, absolutely. Yet they are still <i>fundamentally human.</i> That's the real nightmare of the Nephandi: they're sociopaths of the first order, but they're still <i>us.</i> Technocrats infiltrated by the Nephandi don't have to be overtaken by &quot;demonic influence&quot; any more than a government infiltrated by enemy spies. In a way, that's exactly what a Technephandi situation is.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Heroes die. Hope is a phoenix.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2434-captain-aeon","name":"Captain Aeon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2434_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"203"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-26T09:18:52-07:00","id":528820,"title":"","content":"That's kinda how I feel about it. A brief example would be the fossil fuels executive who (already, in our world, no Nephandi necessarily involved) says, &quot;y'know what, we'll make money off clearing that ancient stand of trees, and if some primitive screwhead is in the way, their tough luck, daddy needs a new boat.&quot; Now imagine the same guy with some yes-man telling him, &quot;obviously, totally, but you know, why wait six months for the people to leave when we can just bulldoze/shoot the recalcitrant?&quot; That's the Nephandic influence, to me. It's not &quot;kill the innocent for Dr. Satan!&quot; so much as &quot;you were going to kinda do this anyway, what's the big deal in taking it a little further?&quot;","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/173-morimaruvik","name":"Morimaruvik","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar173_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"126"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-26T11:19:13-07:00","id":529035,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Morimaruvik</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/528642-m20-technocracy-human-condition-vs-demonic-influence?p=528820#post528820\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">That's kinda how I feel about it. A brief example would be the fossil fuels executive who (already, in our world, no Nephandi necessarily involved) says, &quot;y'know what, we'll make money off clearing that ancient stand of trees, and if some primitive screwhead is in the way, their tough luck, daddy needs a new boat.&quot; Now imagine the same guy with some yes-man telling him, &quot;obviously, totally, but you know, why wait six months for the people to leave when we can just bulldoze/shoot the recalcitrant?&quot; That's the Nephandic influence, to me. It's not &quot;kill the innocent for Dr. Satan!&quot; so much as &quot;you were going to kinda do this anyway, what's the big deal in taking it a little further?&quot;</div> </div> </div> </div>Yep, with the &quot;yes man&quot; working for another corporation, then bailing and getting a big promotion when his rivals get raided by the Feds for, well, mass murder.<br /> <br /> Though I am <i>totally</i> creating a Fallen Etherite who has the brass ones to call himself &quot;Dr. Satan&quot; now. &quot;Behold, my legions of Helltech cyborgs!&quot; (cue lightning and maniacal laughter) <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/cool.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Cool\" smilieid=\"6\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Heroes die. Hope is a phoenix.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2434-captain-aeon","name":"Captain Aeon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2434_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"203"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-26T12:53:12-07:00","id":529139,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Mu_</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/528642-m20-technocracy-human-condition-vs-demonic-influence#post528642\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Please, be cool. I am not purposely throwing strawmen in order to discredit you. I may happen not to understand you and misinterpret your points. It also happens that English is not my native tongue, so sometimes I think to understand something different of what is really said.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'll try my best to accommodate this But that needs to go both ways.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Ok, here is my point. All those are terrible things. But it is simplistic to rely in demonic influence to explain every bad thing in our world. </div> </div> </div>Here's my point:<br /> <br /> Neither I, or M20, are using demonic influence to explain every bad thing in the world of Mage. Demonic influence (which is real in Mage) is part of what makes the world of Mage such a dark place.<br /> <br /> These are, yes, all real things that happen without the Nephandi and their masters. But the real world doesn't have the Traditions, Technocracy, or Disparates pushing back as well.<br /> <br /> I would offer the counterargument that without bad guys like the Marauders and Nephandi in the setting, the fact that some of these issues are actually issues doesn't highlight the human flaws of the Technocracy (or others). It just makes them incompetent cartoonish morons. They aren't humans with flaws; they're flaws in human skins. Addressing the majority of those problems isn't a matter of the Technocracy having trouble implementing new hypertechnology into Consensus to fix things. Almost everything I mentioned is being held back by other factors. The Technocracy has the political power, influence, money, and so on to have easily fixed most of these things; even with human level flaws slowing things down and making things less then perfect. If they haven't, there's two basic choices: either something else is mucking up the works, or they're cartoon villains that can successfully amass huge amounts of power and then are so incompetent at doing anything with it that it's ridiculous.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> In the &quot;Future Fates&quot; box, two of the three options blame the Nephandi, I'll add emphasis to the exact phrases: </div> </div> </div>I think you're vastly overstating the situation of the third option. It is &quot;there's some Nephandi corruption.&quot; which doesn't rest the current state of the Union solely at the Nephandi's feet.<br /> <br /> And even in the first option, where the current state of the Union is the Nephandi's doing, it's still the older Technocrats that let them in.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> This is downtoning for me, because the good tone is where they arrive only by their flaws, only by themselves. </div> </div> </div>I can't see the merit in a world where all bad things are only the cause of your own flaws. It completely removes agency from opposing factions. The Traditions can't subvert the Technocrats in an area, because then it isn't the Technocrat's flaws being the problem. The Marauders can't ruin a Batini plan out of the blue, because then it isn't the Batini's overly cautious Subtle Ones schtick that mucked things up. Etc.<br /> <br /> External forces have to matter too.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Following the mafia analogy, you are admitting flawed Technocrats that can be bribed into doing bad things, but then, if they do that things for the bribe, the ultimate cause of it is pure evil and destroying the world. My point is that the Technocracy created the problem they alone. </div> </div> </div>I think we have a fundamental philosophic disagreement here about the nature of ultimate causes. The tempted, in my view, is the ultimate cause in this. A politician that takes a bribe is the ultimate cause. Not the criminal that bribed them. It was the politician that had the most power, the most agency, and the most say in events. They could have said no to the bribe. Anything that occurs because they said yes to the bribe is ultimately their fault for that choice.<br /> <br /> Similarly, the Technocracy does not lose ultimate blame for their flaws because too many of them said yes to a Nephandus. They could have said no; and didn't.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> To be honest, I don't see why a Nephandus would not use mind control. </div> </div> </div>Because it doesn't really work; especially against the Technocracy (which regularly mind controls its own agents and monitors their behaviors in case they need mental adjustments). Mind magic is hardest on those with strong wills, and mages are universally strong willed people. On top of that, it's not hard to scan for magical effects, and then notice that an agent came back from lunch with a client with an extra effect up.<br /> <br /> On top of all this, it doesn't fit the Nephandus philosophy. There's a reason why they can't mind control mages into the Cauls. Embracing Descent has to be a choice. They'll do everything in their power to stack the odds so you'll make the choices they want, but they believe one of their goals is to get other mages to join them willingly (if under massive duress); not by brute force. A mind-controlled mage is just a pawn likely to be sacrificed for some immediate goal. A corrupted mage that embraces the Nephandi ways is another agent of Descent.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> In real life there humans do not want to destroy the world and they don't want to do evil things just to make a worse world. Some crazy ass can exist, but very very few and he is probably under treatment and totally harmless. </div> </div> </div>Yes. There' are some very, very, evil people out there. And yes, some do just want to inflict as much damage as they can to the world. They are few in numbers, thankfully, but few of them are under any treatment because they are either locked up in prisons or killed because they don't let themselves be captured.<br /> <br /> But the idea that this level of nihilistic sociopathy isn't a human flaw just doesn't hold. It's rare, but it exists. Give it the power of a mage... and you get the Nephandi.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I wanted to say that the Nephandi do not remove the responsibility from the Technocrats. </div> </div> </div>I agree. I'm curious though... why are we going through all this then? Isn't the crux of your argument that the Nephandi do remove it?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Maybe I am wrong, but those Nephandic motivations are normally just excuses that do not make sense when you think carefully about them. So the world is inevitably ending, why accelerate it? And why is that you enjoy the process. </div> </div> </div>I think you're conflating &quot;make sense&quot; with &quot;are rational.&quot;<br /> <br /> Lots of motivations make sense in the context of understanding human behavior, but are irrational motivations at the same time.<br /> <br /> Why accelerate it? Why not. What good is it doing anyone to keep a doomed existence on life support so it can wallow comatose until the machines keeping it alive finally give out? Why extend the pain and suffering of reality when we don't have to? Why not take joy in releasing people from this horrible existence that grinds people up, offers them dreams and then crushes them, and offers no reward for all of this in the end. All so an elite rumored few can &quot;Ascend&quot; and then do nothing for the rest of us.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-27T04:05:51-07:00","id":529737,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Captain Aeon</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/528642-m20-technocracy-human-condition-vs-demonic-influence?p=528809#post528809\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Brief reminder: perhaps the scariest thing about the Nephandi is that they <i>are</i> human.</div> </div> </div> </div>No, they are not. Yes, in the setting they are human, but not in the sense I am speaking here. I am speaking of the human nature of the real world, and Nephandi are not like real world humans.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/528642-m20-technocracy-human-condition-vs-demonic-influence?p=529139#post529139\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">These are, yes, all real things that happen without the Nephandi and their masters. But the real world doesn't have the Traditions, Technocracy, or Disparates pushing back as well.</div> </div> </div> </div>Sure, but as I said, Technocracy is a closer and smarter metaphor.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/528642-m20-technocracy-human-condition-vs-demonic-influence?p=529139#post529139\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I would offer the counterargument that without bad guys like the Marauders and Nephandi in the setting, the fact that some of these issues are actually issues doesn't highlight the human flaws of the Technocracy (or others). It just makes them incompetent cartoonish morons. They aren't humans with flaws; they're flaws in human skins. Addressing the majority of those problems isn't a matter of the Technocracy having trouble implementing new hypertechnology into Consensus to fix things. Almost everything I mentioned is being held back by other factors. The Technocracy has the political power, influence, money, and so on to have easily fixed most of these things; even with human level flaws slowing things down and making things less then perfect. If they haven't, there's two basic choices: either something else is mucking up the works, or they're cartoon villains that can successfully amass huge amounts of power and then are so incompetent at doing anything with it that it's ridiculous.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, I think this is the main theme. As powerful and elightnened mages and technocrat think they are, they are still humans and flawed. More so, some of the human flaws (ambition, pride, fanatism, to be blind to other beliefs) are not disminished but augmented in the Awakened state. Technocracy is corrupted by its own means: power, ambition and a rigid, mathematical and a soulless mindset. They are the consequences of their idieals and methods carried to the extreme.<br /> <br /> By the other hand, the Masses are proving that they are not so willing to be empowered and ascend, or maybe it is that they do not want all to accept the world the Technocracy sells to them. Maybe is that humanity is flawed itself, or maybe the flaws are in the agenda. That's a complex topic and a moral dilemma, unlike the evil Nephandi stopping the Sleeper Ascension.<br /> <br /> Back on our real world, we humans have all the means to make a perfect world. We have an incredible technology that would allow us to cover the neccessities of the entire world. Instead to working to that end, the people amass money and power, make wars, and fight over religion and etnicity topics. If you look on USRR history, you can make a close simil between its government and its bureaucratic body and the Technocracy. They had the means to make a country of happy, well fed and free people, but all that was lost in the inner corruption and the refusal to return the power to the people. The Communist Party corrupted their own ends themselves, without the need of any demonic mage.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/528642-m20-technocracy-human-condition-vs-demonic-influence?p=529139#post529139\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think you're vastly overstating the situation of the third option. It is &quot;there's some Nephandi corruption.&quot; which doesn't rest the current state of the Union solely at the Nephandi's feet.</div> </div> </div> </div>From the phrase &quot;some Nephandi have warped the Union\u2019s purpose&quot; I understand that the reason of the purpose of the Technocracy having been subverted are the Nephandi.<br /> <br /> I insist in that if you propose that there is some Nephandic infiltration, but not to the point of changing the course of the Technocracy into corrupting their own means, we are, in spite of our differences, in the same page.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/528642-m20-technocracy-human-condition-vs-demonic-influence?p=529139#post529139\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I can't see the merit in a world where all bad things are only the cause of your own flaws. [...] External forces have to matter too.</div> </div> </div> </div>It's not like that. Technocracy would be less ruthless if there was not for the threats agaisnt it and humanity. They would not exist in the first place if not for the abuses of the mages. At the same time, the Traditions exist to protect themselves from the Technocracy, and the abuses of the Union forge the hate of the wizards. Bring the other factions and get a system that feeds itself. War makes the worst of us, but that's not the same that having the evil guys creating directly those flaws in order to make the world worst.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/528642-m20-technocracy-human-condition-vs-demonic-influence?p=529139#post529139\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think we have a fundamental philosophic disagreement here about the nature of ultimate causes. The tempted, in my view, is the ultimate cause in this. A politician that takes a bribe is the ultimate cause. Not the criminal that bribed them.</div> </div> </div> </div>I see that, but what I am saying is not to take responsibility from the corrupted, but the end is the one of the corrupter. Blame is no the politician, but the agenda is that of the mafia. In this case, the agenda is to destroy the world or make it worse, which is quite absurd and uninsteresting.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/528642-m20-technocracy-human-condition-vs-demonic-influence?p=529139#post529139\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">What good is it doing anyone to keep a doomed existence on life support so it can wallow comatose until the machines keeping it alive finally give out?</div> </div> </div> </div>That's all we have left. If we are all going to die, what's the hurry?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/528642-m20-technocracy-human-condition-vs-demonic-influence?p=529139#post529139\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> Why not take joy in releasing people from this horrible existence that grinds people up, offers them dreams and then crushes them, and offers no reward for all of this in the end.</div> </div> </div> </div>Because you are inflicting the same issues you are complaining about. Why the joy? If you are ending the world for the good of everybody, why make them suffer in the process? Sorry, still does not make sense.<br /> <br /> I think you are going to have a hard time if you are going to justify the Nephandic point of view rationally.<br /> <br /> <hr /><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Morimaruvik</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/528642-m20-technocracy-human-condition-vs-demonic-influence?p=528805#post528805\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">A &quot;belief&quot; is &quot;the feeling that something is true.&quot; &quot;Religious&quot; is &quot;of or pertaining to religion.&quot; The idea that demons do or do not influence people is a religious belief.<br /> <br /> I don't want to belabor this and derail your thread, honestly, but all you're saying is &quot;the religious belief I stated in my initial post isn't really religious because I'm not religious, despite the fact I opened the thread with my religious beliefs that I say aren't religious because I don't consider myself religious.&quot;<br /> <br /> Demons are a religious concept, not scientific. Any belief regarding them is a religious belief, by definition. A lack of belief one way or the other...a lack of religious belief, in other words...would be more akin to &quot;no fucking clue, I've no experience with what those people are talking about.&quot; Even &quot;I lack experience with demons, therefore I have no reason to believe they exist.&quot;<br /> <br /> But stating as a fact that a religion is wrong about something is, again by definition in English, a religious belief.<br /> <br /> But I feel like I'm repeating myself, and I never intended to derail your thread, so I have nothing more to say on the matter barring further developments.</div> </div> </div> </div>I will say only one more time: I don't have religious beliefs. I don't don't believe &quot;Crhistians are wrong about demons&quot;, I just don't believe in demons, and if that happens to coincide with or contradict with some religious belief, is just coincidence, it does not make it a religious beliefs. Once again, what I think, I do not think to adhere or oppose no religion, you can call it empiric and rational beliefs (although technically its not believing, its not believing, which is different). Such &quot;beliefs&quot; do ar not of nor they pertain to any religion.<br /> <br /> No believing in demons is not a religious belief, as not believing in unicorns is not a mythological belief, not believing in UFOs is not a paranormal belief, not believing in the memory of the water is not a psuedoscience belief and not believing in vampires and mages are not roleplayer beliefs.<br /> <br /> Bald is not a hair color. Not having a dog is not a canine race. The empty glass I have in front of me don't contain a void brand of coffee.<br /> <br /> I don't want to ellaborate on why is important to me to make this clear, because that will force use to enter realms in which someone can feel bad. I never wanted to offend any one nor to defy anyone's beliefs. As I said, I do not follow any relgion, but I do not oppose them either.<br /> <hr />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3673-mu_","name":"Mu_","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3673_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"99"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-27T08:06:27-07:00","id":529959,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Mu_</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/528642-m20-technocracy-human-condition-vs-demonic-influence?p=529737#post529737\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">No, they are not. Yes, in the setting they are human, but not in the sense I am speaking here. I am speaking of the human nature of the real world, and Nephandi are not like real world humans.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think you're going to have to be a bit more thorough in explaining how there aren't really evil people in the world.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Sure, but as I said, Technocracy is a closer and smarter metaphor. </div> </div> </div>It is hard to read this as anything more than, &quot;I like my analogy better because it supports my position more than yours.&quot;<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Well, I think this is the main theme. As powerful and elightnened mages and technocrat think they are, they are still humans and flawed. More so, some of the human flaws (ambition, pride, fanatism, to be blind to other beliefs) are not disminished but augmented in the Awakened state. Technocracy is corrupted by its own means: power, ambition and a rigid, mathematical and a soulless mindset. They are the consequences of their idieals and methods carried to the extreme. </div> </div> </div>Which has zero baring on how Nephandi corruption impacts the moral quandaries of the Technocrats. &quot;Being corrupted by darker forces,&quot; is just another one of those all to human flaws that doesn't get diminished by virtue of being Awakened.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> By the other hand, the Masses are proving that they are not so willing to be empowered and ascend, or maybe it is that they do not want all to accept the world the Technocracy sells to them. Maybe is that humanity is flawed itself, or maybe the flaws are in the agenda. That's a complex topic and a moral dilemma, unlike the evil Nephandi stopping the Sleeper Ascension. </div> </div> </div>This is completely skipping the point. The Union, as an institution, supposedly in the books has the completely mundane power and influence to fix most of the current major global problems in the last 15 years with barely using any effects what-so-ever. It isn't even an issue of changing Consensus, or what humanity wants (because most of humanity wants these things to be fixed). The Union failing to fix these things simply because of their flaws... means they're flaws make them cartoonishly incompetent.<br /> <br /> And please stop with the, &quot;Nephandi are one dimension cartoons that turn all moral dilemmas into jokes because I Mu_ say so.&quot; It adds nothing to the conversation.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Back on our real world, we humans have all the means to make a perfect world. </div> </div> </div>Not unless you have a really low bar for &quot;perfect world.&quot; He have all the means to make a much better world that what we have; but that's all the more evidence that the Union should be able to push us in a better direction if they're unopposed.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> From the phrase &quot;some Nephandi have warped the Union\u2019s purpose&quot; I understand that the reason of the purpose of the Technocracy having been subverted are the Nephandi. </div> </div> </div>You're combining &quot;reason&quot; and &quot;purpose&quot; inappropriately. The reason the Union's purpose got warped is because of the failings of the agents that let the Nephandi in; not the Nephandi themselves. Once the Nephandi are in, they cause problems.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I insist in that if you propose that there is some Nephandic infiltration, but not to the point of changing the course of the Technocracy into corrupting their own means, we are, in spite of our differences, in the same page. </div> </div> </div>I'm not sure this makes us on the same page. It is, however, a valid way to take the Techncracy-Nephandi issue into play. I'm arguing that it isn't the only way to take it into play that doesn't magically destroy key moral and thematic aspects of a Technocracy based game.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> War makes the worst of us, but that's not the same that having the evil guys creating directly those flaws in order to make the world worst. </div> </div> </div>Same? No. But that doesn't mean ones doesn't lead to the other.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I see that, but what I am saying is not to take responsibility from the corrupted, but the end is the one of the corrupter. Blame is no the politician, but the agenda is that of the mafia. </div> </div> </div>I don't think we're going to settle this personally. However I would at least suggest recognizing that the books are written from a philosophic position that you aren't dismissed of ultimate responsibility if you made a pivotal choice in the course of events. It frames things very differently; even if it doesn't go with your personal philosophy.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> That's all we have left. If we are all going to die, what's the hurry? </div> </div> </div>Plenty of people in the US are actively fighting for the right for euthanasia because a slow painful death isn't worth the bits of life you have during it.<br /> <br /> Or, to paraphrase a cultural quote: It is a good deed to extend life, but it is torture to prolong death.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Because you are inflicting the same issues you are complaining about. Why the joy? If you are ending the world for the good of everybody, why make them suffer in the process? Sorry, still does not make sense.<br /> <br /> I think you are going to have a hard time if you are going to justify the Nephandic point of view rationally. </div> </div> </div>Again, you're conflating &quot;make sense,&quot; with &quot;rational decision making.&quot; Those human flaws you keep talking about are born largely out of the fact that humans aren't very rational creatures.<br /> <br /> Let me put this another way: How does it make sense for any Awakened being to not join the Technocracy? Rationally, the Technocracy's human empowerment goals are superior that letting elitist ideologies cause suffering for a select few. If everyone joined the Technocracy it would empower the morally sound Technocrats over the compromised ones by not having a war to fight; and lead to a more tolerant and beneficial Union.<br /> <br /> So how does it make sense to join the Traditions instead?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I will say only one more time: I don't have religious beliefs. </div> </div> </div>I think you're having a language issue here.<br /> <br /> In English, &quot;I don't believe in demons,&quot; is a religious belief; even if it is a statement of disbelief in a religious concept. Or at least a spiritual belief. It's not that what's being said is that you're making your belief decisions based on religious feelings; but that your beliefs about the state of existence of a supernatural phenomenon is a belief about supernatural phenomenon even if your basis is a rational logical dismissal of them.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-27T08:20:59-07:00","id":529972,"title":"","content":"I was thinking that a lot really depends on when the Nephandi corrupted the Technocracy (or the Traditions). If the Nephandi came in on the ground floor, so to speak, (as entry level Order of Reason or Technocrat folk) then I think there is a problem in terms of the Nephandi suddenly being to blame for the Technocrats' bad decisions, because we can't ever really be sure which decisions came from the Nephandi and which from straightforward Technocrats.<br /> <br /> But if the corruption is a fairly recent phenomenon, I don't think the same problem exists. The Technocrats are still 100% to blame for their worst acts in the name of progress or protecting humanity that took place before the corruption. (For example, the worst of colonialism, the more terrible parts of the industrial revolution, the purges, Room 101 and so on.) Really, if you think about it, they're still to blame for anything the Nephandi/Technocrats are doing now, because these higher-ranked members made the choice (knowingly or not) to fall to Nephandic corruption.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2430-kalinara","name":"kalinara","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2430_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"582"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-27T09:23:48-07:00","id":530045,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Firanai</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/528642-m20-technocracy-human-condition-vs-demonic-influence?p=528708#post528708\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think that people are missing the point. The Nephandi don't need control, they just need to corrupt and deceive.<br /> A whisper here and there, the right suggestion at the right time, a compliment to the proud etc...They are the demon on your shoulder convincing you to be selfish because you deserve it, and worst of all they convince you that it was your idea. Bit by bit they feed the worst parts of your character till you become as bad as they are, and you will never suspect a thing.<br /> As for all these rumors and conspiracies of the nephandi ruling over the technocracy and the other factions? they are a lie but that's not the point, the point is that people believe it. The few nephandi infiltrated inside each faction fuel that lie and then sit back and laugh as the the mages tear each other apart and walk towards damnation. It's all a twisted spectacle of paranoia, backstabbing and distrust and the nephandi are having the time of their lives with it.<br /> At least this is the atmosphere that I believe that Satyros was trying to create in the game. It makes for a more interesting and terrifying game. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" /></div> </div> </div> </div>I would love to agree with you, but that's just not what I'm reading in the book. The default setting for the M20 metaplot is that the Nephandi have almost certainly corrupted both of the Union and the Traditions, so the new &quot;good guys&quot; are the Disparates in their struggle against Authority (of all sorts except that which exists internally). It's not just a nudge and wink thing, it's &quot;bad guys have subverted everyone with some modicum of power and authority mage-wise (and always have done for the Union/Order)&quot;. All the little purple boxes aside, that flavor text all over the place in the history the descriptions of the various groups,etc.<br /> <br /> Firiani I think your view on it would jibe better with most chronicle ideas I would jump on running. Not that I wouldn't run or play in a a &quot;Nephandi are eating the world like a rotten peach&quot; game. That would be fun too, but less to my taste in that it does really clamp down on things I saw as the &quot;dinstinguishing downsides&quot; of the Traditions, Union and Nephandi and sorta lumps them all together &quot;pride/hubris goeth before the Fall, and the Fall = Nephandi&quot; and, like Mu (I think), I like my hubris to come in a rainbow of flavors, not just licorice. So SOME Technocrats &amp; Tradition mages (Disparates, Orphans, non-Disparate/non-Orphan organized groups) are corrupted by Nephandi, sure, but it isn't pervasive among the leadership or anywhere else. The Nephandi aren't behind the curtains cackling and rubbing their hands together over their nigh-imminent victory (unless those plucky secret Disparates can get their act together and pull a David on the tag team Goliaths).<br /> <br /> In some ways it seems like its rolling things back to 1st Edition metaplot-wise, just replacing the &quot;faceless evil&quot; of the Technocracy with the &quot;icky tentacles&quot; of the Nephandi. <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-27T10:06:58-07:00","id":530091,"title":"","content":"I don't really see the flavor text pointing to that or the good guys being the Disparates, just the existence of a corruption (especially in the Union) that it is not really known how deep it runs.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1830-history","name":"History","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1830_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"158"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-27T14:13:33-07:00","id":530306,"title":"","content":"Well,.pretty much every description of each Disparate group spends word count in at least one, if not more, places saying that they are part of the alliance because the Technocracy = the Nephandi (and pretty much always has as currently retconned) and for most of them, the Traditions are also considered Nephandi-ridden as well. If you are all banned together, trying to put aside your differences fighting capital E &quot;Evil&quot; together you are pretty much the good guys, based on the whole concept of heroes, antagonists vs. protagonists. <br /> <br /> It outright says that there is corruption in the Inner Council of the Technocracy/Control. In fact, in more than a few places, it says that they are completely corrupted. Hence terms like &quot;Technephandi&quot;. Things are a little more equivocal in concerning the Traditions. They just may be complete idiots and tools and jackasses, but there are repeated references to actual or possible corruption at the highest levels as well. In the Nephandi chapter it states they have the Disparates on the hook as well. Oh, incidentally, a chunk of the mortals as well... effectively defining the Consensus, up to and including Paradox...<br /> <br /> Unless you want to &quot;purple box&quot; your way out of it, that's the core metaplot. It's what's going to be supported in later books when examples are needed, etc.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-27T14:28:51-07:00","id":530318,"title":"","content":"That text is always supposed to be biased. That is the point, there is even one of the purple bars saying that is what the Disparate Alliance &quot;believes&quot;, but it could be not even true. They could be just being misguided or played upon.<br /> <br /> And the main problem is about the Technocracy being corrupted or not, the Traditions corruption is treated as a toolbox possibility to make it even more unexpected to the players more than a default, the main problem the Alliance seems to have with the Traditions is centuries of patronizing.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1830-history","name":"History","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1830_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"158"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-27T20:00:41-07:00","id":530565,"title":"","content":"There is nothing anywhere that states the text has to biased. It is sometimes an interesting way to present the information to do so, but not necessary. <br /> <br /> The main problem is EXACTLY the corruption of the Technocracy. It explicitly states exactly that repeatedly in &quot;Secret Common Ground&quot;. In elaborations on those exact words. They even reverse the order you're describing... the Technocracy weren't imperialistic assholes who were corrupted, they were corrupted ab initio and were imperialistic assholes because of that. <br /> <br /> In addition, the Stereotype of the Technocracy for the Batini, Kopa Loei, Taftani. Templars and Wu Lung explicitly state they are Nephandi and it's heavily implied with the Children of Knowledge, Hollow Ones, Ngoma and Orphans (where the Technocracy is called evil! evil! evil!, in terms that imply more than just mere asshole-ishness, capital &quot;E&quot; Evil). <br /> <br /> GIven, it is all a toolbox. Take what you like. Leave what you like. Amend and create at will. That's how the metaplot has always been. But, that does not diminish the power of the metalplot. It is pervasive and game-shaping. The Disparates and the Nephandi sections of the book put the game firmly in the &quot;everyone is corrupt in this world of shit except for the Disparates and the Marauders&quot;. In this universe, you play someone other than those two you are &quot;little folks&quot; fighting &quot;the Man&quot; (who bears a striking resemblance to Snidley Whiplash twirling his mustache) or you are ignorant of what's really going on behind the curtain, since all your bosses (or boss's bosses or boss's colleagues) are tainted and corrupted. And so are those of the guys on the other side too!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-28T00:51:36-07:00","id":530760,"title":"","content":"There's also nothing stating that the information put forth HAS to be 100% accurate fact either.<br /> <br /> I think it would be all the more interesting if the Disparates are dead wrong, and were in fact lead by the Nephandi into thinking everyone else was compromised in order to round up yet another group of mages into the Ascension War. That it's all a con to increase the level and complexity of the fighting. The Nephandi get to sit back and watch the various factions eat each other, all without the Fallen lifting a finger.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-28T03:48:00-07:00","id":530849,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ajax</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/528642-m20-technocracy-human-condition-vs-demonic-influence?p=530565#post530565\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">There is nothing anywhere that states the text has to biased. It is sometimes an interesting way to present the information to do so, but not necessary.</div> </div> </div> </div>But as I said you, in this specific case there is even a purple bar directly saying that what it is said about the infiltration it is just what the Disparate Alliance believes and it maybe is not even true.<br /> <br /> Also, I think you are talking for granted a lot of stuff in the book when it is also said a lot that the Nephandi love to play with paranoia and maybe they are not really infiltrated to those levels (or they are just in some factions and not in the other ones) and are using that fear on their favor. This thing about the Nephandi is not even something new from M20, it comes from old books.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1830,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1830-history","user":"History","date":". 05-28-2015, 06:59 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1830-history","name":"History","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1830_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"158"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-28T10:30:53-07:00","id":531119,"title":"","content":"It is definitely a new thing at the level at which it is being set. Sure the Nephandi were out there and you can have them as involved or not in your chronicle, but they were out there being devious and influencing things here and there, NOT running both of the major of factions and, effectively, according to the Nephandi section pretty much EVERYTHING (which, since it strongly discourages Nephandi PCs is inherently less biased as it is more of an overall/ST resource than a player perspective tool).<br /> <br /> And that is exactly one of the major stances the M20 rules are taking. The &quot;Disparates Unite &amp; Take Down the Nephandi (meaning everyone else)&quot; may even be the primary stance from which the game metaplot will progress. It certainly seems backed up far beyond merely the write-up of just the Disparates. Sure there were threads here and there in previous editions - most of the previously addressed Technocracy/Nephandi crossover was Syndicate and the occasional VE. And those threads can be woven together to make up this story, but theyweren't core to the metaplot. They are now. And metaplot, while eminently toss-able, does have an effect not only on how the rules are written for this book, but will have an effect on how future books are created. <br /> <br /> Sure, I CAN say that my Technocracy is not riddled with Nephandi like swiss cheese (if swiss cheese had a black hole at the center of the wheel), but if a Technephandi is the baseline supposition, any Technocracy book is going to be written with that in mind. NPCs will be constructed with a mind to their level of complicity in the corruption. Rules will be in place to develop the Nephandi influence further. There will be a few purple boxes here and there to help me out to de-rot the Technocracy, but the balance of anything Technocratic from this point forward looks like it is going to assume a rotten core and rotting peripheries with the PCs interacting with those assumptions in some way. <br /> <br /> It's definitely a change from the Technocracy presented the GttT in more than trivial ways. <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-28T11:00:06-07:00","id":531137,"title":"","content":"The Nephandi part is written to play and amp the whole paranoia angle. It doesn't matter that it discourages Nephandi PCs or not, it is written to enforce that thing about the Nephandi being manipulators and the WWII situation repeating again. That doesn't mean that you have to take it for granted, it is there to create that specific feeling about them and to make it scary and not just ooga booga cultists.<br /> <br /> Yes, there are probably Nephandi in the Inner Circle (that's old news) as far the default option (and that one basically is that they run the Technocracy to some degree with their manipulations, not that they completely run EVERYTHING as you are implying) is concerned, but you are just overreacting to something that is not even supposed to be set in the stone and told in a biased manner so the ST can use it however he wants.<br /> <br /> And before you even exaggerated the whole situation by placing the Disparate Alliance as the sole heroes against the Nephandi, when the part about the latter says that they are infiltrated even in the Disparates too. If anything, the default approach of the book is actually shaping into a Disparates, Traditions and the idealistic side of the Technocracy against the Nephandi. I'm even pretty sure that Satyros commented that was how he saw the Mage future coming in an interview.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1830,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1830-history","user":"History","date":". 05-28-2015, 02:08 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1830-history","name":"History","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1830_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"158"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-28T21:43:14-07:00","id":531726,"title":"","content":"Oh I perfectly agree that the &quot;Nephandi Controlling Everyone&quot; plot will be reinforced and supported in the metaplot in books to come. It's one of my major complaints of WoD Revised in general. Sure, you can ignore the metaplot all you want, but the designers aren't going to support your decision with content. If you don't like the metaplot, the books have little to offer you.<br /> <br /> I'd just like a little more ambiguity is all. The players don't know just how far the corruption goes, and it scares the hell out of them.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-29T06:01:58-07:00","id":531905,"title":"","content":"My only real problem with the Nephandi as currently presented is that their level of success seems to require too much infallibility, both in terms of planning and in terms of coverup. I do like this idea that they get caught out all the time, though, and are just good at using scapegoats to make their slipups seem like only small scale infestations.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-29T08:06:41-07:00","id":531961,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>History</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/528642-m20-technocracy-human-condition-vs-demonic-influence?p=531137#post531137\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The Nephandi part is written to play and amp the whole paranoia angle. It doesn't matter that it discourages Nephandi PCs or not, it is written to enforce that thing about the Nephandi being manipulators and the WWII situation repeating again. That doesn't mean that you have to take it for granted, it is there to create that specific feeling about them and to make it scary and not just ooga booga cultists.<br /> <br /> Yes, there are probably Nephandi in the Inner Circle (that's old news) as far the default option (and that one basically is that they run the Technocracy to some degree with their manipulations, not that they completely run EVERYTHING as you are implying) is concerned, but you are just overreacting to something that is not even supposed to be set in the stone and told in a biased manner so the ST can use it however he wants.<br /> <br /> And before you even exaggerated the whole situation by placing the Disparate Alliance as the sole heroes against the Nephandi, when the part about the latter says that they are infiltrated even in the Disparates too. If anything, <i><b>the default approach of the book is actually shaping into a Disparates, Traditions and the idealistic side of the Technocracy against the Nephandi. I'm even pretty sure that Satyros commented that was how he saw the Mage future coming in an interview</b></i>.</div> </div> </div> </div>See that last part? That's the metaplot that people are having issues with. It's not setting neutral. It will shape all things to come. There might be purple boxes here and there, but if you aren't into the Nephandi having essentially (and apparently effortlessly) won and the game being a stand against their encroaching darkness, then you're going to have to some work with everything from this point forward. That's from M20 right out of the box henceforth. And I anticipate less and less purple boxes as time goes by without some major line control on future writers. So, YES, it's not an exaggeration, it's a comment. And it's a concern. And it's a <i>real </i>concern, as it will affect the utility of future products for people who don't want to play along. AND it's also not a super-big-interest-ending catastrophe. It's something to note, sigh, and move on to planning your first M20 Chronicle if you don't want to have the Nephandi play that role. <br /> <br /> Here's the current set-up and why there might be some problems with it:<br /> <b>Core conceit: </b> It's us (for various values of &quot;us&quot; from single cabals all the way up) against the Nephandi. <br /> <b>Traditions:</b> These guys are almost utterly clueless about the important fight. They are all about instrospection and image modification. Basically idiots if not tools. Still stuck in us vs. Technocracy and no one else is important. Nephandi? Kill them and Maurauders when you see them, then go back to exploring your feelings about our checkered past and how we can all get along from now on.<br /> <b>Technocracy:</b> Corrupted. It will affect most, if not all Technocracy games set up in M20 metaplot as previously described.<br /> <b>Disparates:</b> The lonely group of mismatched, misfit heroes in the new drama. Despite what you keep saying, the book itself does not back you up. The Disparates are joined together by two things - people have been mean to them so they have shared anger over that and, oh, that is largely the fault of the Nephandi. There is no intention by any of the groups to work with or play nice with the Traditions or the Technocracy in this fight as per all the Stereotype sections and the text proper for each and every one. Some slant more to the &quot;we can all get along because they were mean to us&quot; but the &quot;Nephandi must be destroyed&quot; is much stronger. Witness the fact the Ahl-i-Batin are considered (consider themselves) the creators of the new alliance and they did specifically to aid in there ages old fight against the Nephandi. (I guess the Hollow Ones also believe they created the alliance, but they are pretty vague in the head overall, so that's worth what it's worth).<br /> <b>The Brucato Ideal of a Disparates/Traditions/Technocracy Controloyalists Alliance</b>: Specifically and definitely reccomended AGAINST in the text on working together to set up groups. In fact, a starting cabal based on that ideal was addressed as a &quot;Resevoir Dogs style&quot; bloodbath from the get-go.<br /> <br /> I think it's fine as an option, but it's being pushed a little to strongly for me and, I feel, for the OP, specifically with the Technocracy, and for some others who have posted here as well. Take a look at the playing a Technocracy game thread and you'll see the Consensus (hardy-har-har) seems to be, if you want to play a Union game that will not have to address the Technephandi plot either head on or as a major tanget, M20 is &quot;rules excellent&quot; but stronger source material for plotting purposes is the GttT and, depending on how you want your metaplot to swing, the Rev. Convention books.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-29T09:03:23-07:00","id":531990,"title":"","content":"It's just the setting advancing, but it's not really that pushy unless you follow the books word by word. And if you do, you even have some stuff about how the Disparate Alliance can get ugly and disband really quick if some stuff goes on (and they being weak and disorganised compared to the two big factions), so it's not all roses and flowers with them as you try to paint.<br /> <br /> I'm pretty sure than not all the books in the future will be about that metaplot but just a few here and there. Also, nothing is really stopping you from discarding it, just an inability to do anything else but to follow a default premise in the book.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1830,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1830-history","user":"History","date":". 05-29-2015, 12:28 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1830-history","name":"History","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1830_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"158"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-29T15:13:02-07:00","id":532377,"title":"","content":"Please read through my previous posts carefully as I repeatedly stated that I was more than willing to adopt all, some or none of the parts of the new overarching metaplot, depending on the needs of whichever chronicle we happen to be running next. <br /> <br /> Underestimating the role that metaplot has on shaping future books is not something borne out by any evidence of past White Wolf or Onyx Path game production. White Wolf games have been metaplot heavy from the get go. The rules and the metaplot have always been tightly interwoven. When the rules changed the metaplot turned and vice-versa. I doubt there is very much chance that we will be seeing metaplot in only an occasional book here and there. That's just not how things have ever been done with these games. In fact, that's one of the things many players LOVE about White Wolf games. The STORY to it all. It can make it worthwhile to buy ALL the books even if you can't play the game. Just to keep up with the soap opera.<br /> <br /> So, I COULD have the Disparates fall apart, which, all things being equal, they probably should.... if Yugoslavia couldn't work, this kind of plurality of multiply dysfunctional ethnocentrics is a disaster waiting to happen. Particularly since they are all running around trying to keep it secret. If I DO, I'm betting pretty much 100% what happens in my game will be close to 180 degrees off of what would be happening in later supplements<br /> <br /> Going back to the OP (again), the centrality of the Technephandi (and Nephandi overall) just isn't to a some of people's taste. To them and to me (mostly, depending on the game) the Nephandi were one cautionary tale and the Technocracy represented another. Now they represent the same thing. <br /> <br /> And, yes, of course, they/we/I can ignore it. That's a given. Golden Rule (of White Wolf games) and all. But the M20 setting was presented as being, if not meta-plot neutral, more meta-plot optional or even meta-plot toolbox/sandbox fashion than it turned out to be in some ways. Not that I am dissing the purple boxes. Awesome, overall. I would probably just want a little more shoved up into them.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-29T16:32:10-07:00","id":532497,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ajax</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/528642-m20-technocracy-human-condition-vs-demonic-influence?p=532377#post532377\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Underestimating the role that metaplot has on shaping future books is not something borne out by any evidence of past White Wolf or Onyx Path game production. White Wolf games have been metaplot heavy from the get go.</div> </div> </div> </div>How does that work?<br /> <br /> The pre-20th cWOD and Aeon-verse were certainly metaplot heavy.<br /> <br /> Exalted eschewed metaplot from the get go. There has been lots of setting expansion over the course of two full editions, lots of history revealed, and a new edition on the way. And yet still not advancement of the basic set up of the plot/setting from the default &quot;now&quot; as canon in any books.<br /> <br /> Scion doesn't have a metaplot in a technical sense. Though you can make an argument that it uses metaplot as a framing device for introducing the higher tiers of play in a very different fashion from how metaplot was used in cWOD and Aeon.<br /> <br /> The nWOD has likewise avoided metaplot.<br /> <br /> The 20th anniversary books have addressed the old metaplot. But with V20 and W20 both a few books in now, there's no real new metaplot developments. Again, there's been addressing of old metaplot with new material. But they haven't established new metaplot that's developed in the new editions.<br /> <br /> So... how's this history (esp. Onyx Path's history) something that indicates 20th will end up another metaplot heavy set of books?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-30T09:58:26-07:00","id":533318,"title":"","content":"This doesn't seem right thread, since it's really not on topic, but here goes:<br /> <br /> Scion and NWoD definitely had meta-plot, though the hand was much lighter than with CWOD. Scion with the overall framing device of a new Titanomachy. That was a set of boxes into which everything in the game was shoehorned and it was a set of boxes for which several of the mythologies depicted were ill-suited to shoe-horning. The rules definitely took this metaplot into account all over the place. (Why aren't the gods running around the mortal world? Fate-binding and Legendary Deeds.... &lt;rules, rules, rules&gt; So which Titan (implicitly tied to which General Purview) is matched up with which pantheon and who gets picked to be Avatars... etc. etc. <br /> <br /> The NWOD it is more background (pre-God Machine), but there is a coherent world out there and while not all the games plug together perfectly, there are continuous narratives discernable across several games.... Mummy and Geist and Mage and Prometheean have different views of the same Underworld which lock together into a coherent storyline.... some devastating world cracking prehistoric event shaping the way spirit and material interact from Werewolf and Mage and Hunter..... how the Watchtowers of Mage relate, and how they don',t with Geist, Changeling, etc. There may not have been the eschatology of CWOD, but there was definably something that linked them all up. And that was reflective of and a reflection of the fact the rules were designed to work together for the most part. That's metaplot and it's rules affecting. Those are rules and they affect metaplot.<br /> <br /> I have stayed out of Exalted since it started (aside from playing it a couple of times) My PhD dissertation topic was Bronze Age related so the setting was grating to me at the beginning (like being a lawyer and watching TV shows or movies based around legal matters.... I just start shouting &quot;Objection!&quot; at the TV screen....). Then it swtiched over completely to a more anime-simulation vein and that wasn't so much to my taste. So I can't bring anything to illustrate from there, if it even exists. <br /> <br /> CWOD used metaplot narrative repeatedly across the games that had more than one edition to describe how the world changing was reflected in the rules changing or by bringing forward some new aspect of metaplot to represent the aspect of the rules changes. Or having some explanation of why this and that don't jibe across different editions of the game. (Celerity in DA vs. Celerity everywhen else). That's a feedback loop, of course, as the metaplot would also drive rules changes as much as it reflected them. <br /> <br /> There IS a metaplot in M20. It's going to affect what is subsequently put forwards. We've even heard it straight from the horses mouths (as it were), when developers start talking about things like &quot;the Grand Alliance&quot; of the few good Technocrats, the part of the Traditions that is able to pull their collective heads out of its collective asses and the &quot;New [Fragile] Hope&quot; of the Disparate Alliance vs. the Nephandi as &quot;the new vision&quot; for the game. <br /> <br /> Now, has M20 done BETTER than any CWOD game in providing a tool box with which to customize the level of old and new metaplot are in play? Sure. Absolutely. Thanks to all the gods that may be for the advent of the Purple Boxes. But the new metaplot will have an effect. WW and Onyx Path don't produce games that are almost complete crunch, like say, Fate or Pathfinder or D&amp;D Xth Ed. Those system are metaplot neutral. You can take those rules and pretty much do anything with them. Which has its own good points and bad points. But the rich lushness of White Wolf has always been that the rules are meant to part of a game with the story and setting built in. <br /> <br /> To that end, perhaps it would be cool to explore what to use and not to use from M20 and previous materials to create Technocracy games with less of a Technephandi feel? Can you do it with M20 proper? If so, are there simple tweaks needed? How about integrating it more closely with the Technocracy-thru-Rosier-Colored-Mirrorshades of GttT? What would be the points to hit on? How about the role of the Rev. Convention books? What does it mean to separate out the &quot;distinguishable downsides&quot; of each Faction and throwing each one of them up as a warning saying &quot;Do Not Enter&quot; (albeit usually hidden behind some vegetation, sometimes knocked down and half-buried...)<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-30T20:59:33-07:00","id":533982,"title":"","content":"You... seem to be using the world &quot;metaplot&quot; in a very different sense that how the word is generally defined.<br /> <br /> Building a cohesive world/setting is not metaplot. How it interacts with rules is certainly part of its quality, but not what it is or is not.<br /> <br /> Metaplot is advancing the time frame of the game forward with in game events as new books come out. This is why the nWOD doesn't have metaplot. There's no &quot;now&quot; that turns into the &quot;past&quot; when new books are released. Events in books are optional tools that have no effect on anything but the backstory. VtR 2e is not VtR 1e but 10 years later with changes to the setting based on 10 years of supplement events.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-31T09:31:16-07:00","id":534483,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/528642-m20-technocracy-human-condition-vs-demonic-influence?p=533982#post533982\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You... seem to be using the world &quot;metaplot&quot; in a very different sense that how the word is generally defined.<br /> <br /> Building a cohesive world/setting is not metaplot. How it interacts with rules is certainly part of its quality, but not what it is or is not.<br /> <br /> Metaplot is advancing the time frame of the game forward with in game events as new books come out. This is why the nWOD doesn't have metaplot. There's no &quot;now&quot; that turns into the &quot;past&quot; when new books are released. Events in books are optional tools that have no effect on anything but the backstory. VtR 2e is not VtR 1e but 10 years later with changes to the setting based on 10 years of supplement events.</div> </div> </div> </div>Different definitions then. I think it's pretty narrow to think of metaplot as only being a situation in which a game has an intrinsic story and then that story is changed by the continuing publication of additional supplements. Metaplot is having a story embedded into the setting. There are rules systems out there that are attached to NO story whatsoever. Most of those games also have an attached setting with a metaplot in your parlance. But that is not necessary to play the game and without it, you are almost effectively not playing that game at all (more using the rules system to model something that most wouldn't perceive as being within the confines of the game if discribed.... i.e. &quot;We are playing Mage but we're all the children of gods! vs. &quot;I like the basic idea of Scion but I hate the system, so I'm taking the magic system of Mage to represent the growing power of a young deity&quot;). I would conditionally agree that NWOD was an attempt to do what you are saying, have a setting with no progressive narrative. It worked exactly as long as the core book was out and before you read VtR and fell to pieces completely with God-Machine (though the option for a NWOD game is still out there, I guess). But Scion is a game RULED by its intrinsic narrative for better and for worse. <br /> <br /> And WW/OP has always linked changes in the rules to the incremental changes made in the overall narrative. Hence the feeling/concern/apprehension (by some) that, if you don't want to play in the new sandbox, you are going to have more work to do to port over any new material. It's not a new thing. And it's not just on the player side either. (Witness the dance over the True Black Hand in Vampire). <br /> <br /> I'm not sure what we're discussing or why at this point. It seems pretty esoteric considering the concerns of the OP. Hence my attempt to see if there was any interest in seeing if people were thinking about ways to de-Nephandi the Technephandi, and if so, how and why.<br /> <br /> If you LIKE the Technephandi plotline that's totally cool. I don't think it's bad. It's a coherent narrative that makes a lot of sense based on previous plot threads, coupons dropped, etc. I might use it to some degree, depending on the next chronicle we play. If we do a pure-Union game, I am definitely going to have to tackle how much of it I want to include, if any. And we might... It's on the table as a possibility. So I am seeing what those who might want their Technocracy to have less of a Nephandi flavor are thinking, as I am not sure that I want that to be the dominant narrative.<br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-31T12:37:29-07:00","id":534722,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ajax</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/528642-m20-technocracy-human-condition-vs-demonic-influence?p=534483#post534483\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Different definitions then.</div> </div> </div> </div>The problem is a lot of what you're saying hinges on your definition of metaplot, which seems significantly different from what most people mean when they say it that it has a fairly negative impact on our ability to discuss things.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I think it's pretty narrow to think of metaplot as only being a situation in which a game has an intrinsic story and then that story is changed by the continuing publication of additional supplements. </div> </div> </div>What you think about a definition of a word really isn't relevant to how the word is used by a significant number of other people. I mean, Googling metaplot and looking at things like Wikipedia or TVTropes and this is what both say metaplot distills down to.<br /> <br /> The &quot;narrowness&quot; is inherent it the term being functional as a concept we can agree on and discuss. A broad wish-washy term that has no central conceptual meaning isn't useful for discussing RPGs because it just adds confusion.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Metaplot is having a story embedded into the setting. </div> </div> </div>What's the difference between metaplot and plot then?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> It worked exactly as long as the core book was out and before you read VtR and fell to pieces completely with God-Machine (though the option for a NWOD game is still out there, I guess). </div> </div> </div>Besides the inclusion of a small amount of metaplot to deal with VtR having material discussing New Orleans that came out shortly before Hurricane Katrina, and what to do with such a massive real world event and that information, what metaplot has happened in the nWOD exactly?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> But Scion is a game RULED by its intrinsic narrative for better and for worse. </div> </div> </div>This is fairly backwards. Scion is a game about intrinsic narratives. There isn't an actual metaplot going on. The story of the signature characters that progresses through the three core books... has no impact on the game setting. If all of them don't exist in your instance of Scion there are not setting or rules holes created. Nothing in the setting requires any of them to exist. The narrative framework the progresses through the core books is a vital part of the game (since its a game about exploring that narrative framework) but a framework isn't a plot by itself.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> And WW/OP has always linked changes in the rules to the incremental changes made in the overall narrative. </div> </div> </div>WW did this a lot for some of the game lines. But there's a difference in, say, Exalted 2e changing some of the base line assumptions of the game and having new mechanics to deal with that, and having a Clan Weakness change because of an event in a previous supplement.<br /> <br /> OP hasn't demonstrated any notable pattern of this that I can point to.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Hence my attempt to see if there was any interest in seeing if people were thinking about ways to de-Nephandi the Technephandi, and if so, how and why. </div> </div> </div>I have... no interest in that. If I want to run Technocrats without the Nephandi having made such massive in roads into corrupting them. I will. I haven't seen anything from OP that makes me think I'll have an issues with this in M20. It just means using the Future Fates options where the Nephandi haven't gotten that far. Changing the Technephandi concept isn't necessary.<br /> <br /> The issue in all this seems to be far more preconceptions and confirmation bias than any real problem in running the games has we want. The old cWOD books were metaplot heavy enough it burnt a lot of us, so we're nervous about it rearing up into becoming the same beast again. Worries about M20 doing the same thing that the old cWOD books (despite not seeing any of that in the V20 and M20 supplements), and seeing the Technephandi story seed as &quot;dominant&quot; in a 700 page book that talks about so many other game options, don't really seem to be things we need to fix by altering what's in the book.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-31T17:53:58-07:00","id":534981,"title":"","content":"I'm not really interested in changing the book. I'm sorry if somehow you've misread my posts and gotten that impression. Please go back and re-read the many times I have stated that in multiple different ways. I like the book just fine as it is. Did it accomplish my personal wish list of desired changes? Absolutely not. Did I expect it to? Absolutely not.<br /> <br /> I did see the way the Technocracy was being handled and the options offered up and saw them as lacking, since the Technephandi ARE part of the metaplot and that WILL affect how the Technocracy in future products. That's it. I'm not asking Brucato to rewrite it to please me or the other posters who feel similarly. I'm interested in exploring what other options didn't get into the purple box and what people think the options are and how to affect them (which does seem to be a concern as other aspects of this very question are being explored in other threads, i.e. starting a Union game, discussing what to do about Threat Null, etc.) <br /> <br /> Currently, I see this exchange as off topic. I've been trying to either move this thread back towards the discussion points of the OP or let it die a natural death. If you would like to engage in a debate of the ins and outs of plotting and metanarratives in static vs. dynamics forms, I'd be happy do so in a separate thread.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-02T11:17:24-07:00","id":537229,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/528642-m20-technocracy-human-condition-vs-demonic-influence?p=529959#post529959\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I think you're going to have to be a bit more thorough in explaining how there aren't really evil people in the world.</div> </div> </div> </div>No, no, no. There ARE evil people in the world. The thing is that those people do evil not for the sake of evil, nor for make Satan happy, nor to destroy the world, but to take profit for that evil.<br /> <br /> Three considerations about real life evil:<br /> <br /> * As said, evil people do evil things because those things are convenient for them, not just to be evil.<br /> <br /> * Very, very few people in the world admit they are evil, even to themselves. They always find moral excuses to justify their actions.<br /> <br /> * Some people are evil for some things and good for others. There is that boss that crushes his employees, but is a good friend and a really good father for his children, and there is that filantropic man that sacrifices his time and energy for a good cause, but treat his own family like shit.<br /> <br /> Let me give you an extreme and controversial example. Pablo Escobar was the biggest drug lord of all times. He was responsible of hundreds of death, including police, politics, judges, journalists an all kind of people. Yet, he is still considered a hero in Medell\u00edn (his home town) and for other people in Colombia. You know why? Because he was good to this people. He build houses for the needed, and he really helped his home town. He is known to be also very good to his family.<br /> <br /> Even the TV series &quot;El Patr\u00f3n del Mal&quot; (&quot;The Boss of Evil&quot;), which is produced by the sons of two of the people he killed, show his good deeds (along with the evil ones).<br /> <br /> This is how evil people are in real life, persons of light and shadows.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/528642-m20-technocracy-human-condition-vs-demonic-influence?p=529959#post529959\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Which has zero baring on how Nephandi corruption impacts the moral quandaries of the Technocrats. &quot;Being corrupted by darker forces,&quot; is just another one of those all to human flaws that doesn't get diminished by virtue of being Awakened.<br /> [...]<br /> And please stop with the, &quot;Nephandi are one dimension cartoons that turn all moral dilemmas into jokes because I Mu_ say so.&quot; It adds nothing to the conversation.<br /> [...]<br /> You're combining &quot;reason&quot; and &quot;purpose&quot; inappropriately. The reason the Union's purpose got warped is because of the failings of the agents that let the Nephandi in; not the Nephandi themselves. Once the Nephandi are in, they cause problems.<br /> [...]<br /> I don't think we're going to settle this personally. However I would at least suggest recognizing that the books are written from a philosophic position that you aren't dismissed of ultimate responsibility if you made a pivotal choice in the course of events. It frames things very differently; even if it doesn't go with your personal philosophy.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'll try to clarify this for once. I am not taking the responsability from the corrupted, by in the nephandic version, even if each one is to blame for his part, the all evil Nephandi are the final cause for all, so all the explanation ends in ultimate evil, instead of the more complex causes that we (or the character) could see, consider and discuss.<br /> <br /> Still, when there aren't Nephandi to blame, characters can discuss or decide if the ends justify the means and if they are doing the right thing or not. When Nephandi are in charge, there is no such discussion, the means are wrong beacause they are done at the end by Nephandi who want to do evil and detroy the world.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/528642-m20-technocracy-human-condition-vs-demonic-influence?p=529959#post529959\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">This is completely skipping the point. The Union, as an institution, supposedly in the books has the completely mundane power and influence to fix most of the current major global problems in the last 15 years with barely using any effects what-so-ever. It isn't even an issue of changing Consensus, or what humanity wants (because most of humanity wants these things to be fixed). The Union failing to fix these things simply because of their flaws... means they're flaws make them cartoonishly incompetent.<br /> [...]<br /> Not unless you have a really low bar for &quot;perfect world.&quot; He have all the means to make a much better world that what we have; but that's all the more evidence that the Union should be able to push us in a better direction if they're unopposed.</div> </div> </div> </div>The Technocracy is really, overwhelming powerful. But, did you stop to think what are the facing?<br /> <br /> * The Traditions.<br /> * The Nephandi.<br /> * The Marauders.<br /> * Primal creatures that inhabited the Earth before man (aka Mythic Beasts).<br /> * Unspeakable horrors from beyond.<br /> * Aliens.<br /> * All kind of supernatural creatures.<br /> * The chaotic nature of the Masses.<br /> * The egoistical (but not necessarily Nephandic) Technocrats.<br /> * The stupid Technocrats (every organization has stupid people).<br /> * The difference of opinion beetween Conventions.<br /> * The difference of opinion beetween individuals.<br /> * The typical human and mage flaws.<br /> <br /> There is even a theory in M20 explaining why the Masses reject the good things that Technocracy brings, see &quot;The Shitty Factor&quot; in page .<br /> <br /> So, we have it again. I see a phenomenon and my explanation includes lots of factors that conflicts each other, and each one is arguable.<br /> <br /> You see the same phenomenon and your explanation is &quot;Nephandi did this because they are evil&quot;.<br /> <br /> Don't you see why I say that this ends with the moral dilemmas (and other kind of dilemmas)?<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/528642-m20-technocracy-human-condition-vs-demonic-influence?p=529959#post529959\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Plenty of people in the US are actively fighting for the right for euthanasia because a slow painful death isn't worth the bits of life you have during it.<br /> <br /> Or, to paraphrase a cultural quote: It is a good deed to extend life, but it is torture to prolong death.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, but when one administer Euthanasia it gives the sick man a drug that ends his life peacefully and without pain, instead of throwing the pacient to a meat grinder. Nephandi are deliberately cruel.<br /> <br /> More so, when a doctor has to end the life of his pacient, he does it without pleasure, maybe even with tears in his face (I would cry if I had to do such a thing). Nephandi are described as sadistic, which conflicts with the moral excuses presented in the books.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/528642-m20-technocracy-human-condition-vs-demonic-influence?p=529959#post529959\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Let me put this another way: How does it make sense for any Awakened being to not join the Technocracy? Rationally, the Technocracy's human empowerment goals are superior that letting elitist ideologies cause suffering for a select few. If everyone joined the Technocracy it would empower the morally sound Technocrats over the compromised ones by not having a war to fight; and lead to a more tolerant and beneficial Union.<br /> <br /> So how does it make sense to join the Traditions instead?</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm glad you made me this question :-)<br /> <br /> Let's suppose there are not Nephandi in the Technocracy. It is still an organization that supposedly works for the Empowerment of Humanity.<br /> <br /> Still, the Traditions see, with reason, that the Technocracy has decided which reality is meant for the humanity, without asking them. And their methods, they aren't the bests.<br /> <br /> But the Technocracy see that the world of the Traditionals would be a chaotic one, a world of darkness in which the powerful mages would have advantage over the common men.<br /> <br /> The Council replies that the world of the Technocracy is one of submission to the union, of pens\u00e9e unique (&quot;single thought&quot;), where magick and wonder are banned from the world.<br /> <br /> What do we have here? It is a moral dilemma. A mage is confronted with a decision: &quot;Should I fight for individual thought or for the good of all humanity? Which world is better (or worse)?&quot;. Each character has to answer this difficult question (and many others) and each one will answer in a different way (the joy of Role-playing).<br /> <br /> This is the kind of conflict I like, and this can be the interesting point about a game that is about which Reality we want. Putting the Nephandi on charge, returns the game to the first edition of good guys and bad guys.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/528642-m20-technocracy-human-condition-vs-demonic-influence?p=529959#post529959\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think you're having a language issue here.<br /> <br /> In English, &quot;I don't believe in demons,&quot; is a religious belief; even if it is a statement of disbelief in a religious concept. Or at least a spiritual belief. It's not that what's being said is that you're making your belief decisions based on religious feelings; but that your beliefs about the state of existence of a supernatural phenomenon is a belief about supernatural phenomenon even if your basis is a rational logical dismissal of them.</div> </div> </div> </div>I will try to set it clear. The other user defined religious as &quot;of or pertaining of religion&quot;. Since my beliefs do not pertain nor are build in opposition of any religion, they don't have a religious nature.<br /> <br /> When you say &quot;my religious belief&quot; you are saying that my ideas, which I get from my experience and reasoning, are of the same nature of the ideas that some people get from cultural inheritance and faith. And since I have decided to make my own ideas out of reasoning and experience, I find offensive that you try to put them in the same category that those who are maintained only by or mainly by faith.<br /> <br /> I don't want to explore this deeper (I did not want to got this far) because some people can get offended by these topics, but I won't let this go.<br /> <br /> For a more ellaborated explanation of why lack of reiligion is not religion, see my previous post.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3673-mu_","name":"Mu_","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3673_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"99"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-02T13:27:36-07:00","id":537384,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Mu_</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/528642-m20-technocracy-human-condition-vs-demonic-influence?p=537229#post537229\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The thing is that those people do evil not for the sake of evil, nor for make Satan happy, nor to destroy the world, but to take profit for that evil.</div> </div> </div> </div>&quot;Profit&quot; can be malicious psychology as well as rational profit. And yeah, there are people that do evil for the sake of a supernatural entity's supposed joy (not just Satan), because the profit is that they feel good having done so. Some people get off on destruction, pain, and suffering. Doing things that cause those is profit for them.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> As said, evil people do evil things because those things are convenient for them, not just to be evil. </div> </div> </div>This is patently untrue as a universal statement. Serial killers, for example, generally have elaborate and inconvenient fantasies that drive their actions. They might not be evil for the sake of evil (at least from their perspective) but they aren't doing things because it is easier for them. This is true for most &quot;mundane&quot; and &quot;small&quot; evils, it doesn't get into the complexities of those that commit crimes on much larger scales.<br /> <br /> Or hell, look at rich kleptomaniacs. People worth millions of dollars that steal. Why? Because they can. Because it's a rush. It's exciting and taboo. It's also clearly irrational and stupid.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Very, very few people in the world admit they are evil, even to themselves. They always find moral excuses to justify their actions. </div> </div> </div>As do most Nephandi.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Some people are evil for some things and good for others. There is that boss that crushes his employees, but is a good friend and a really good father for his children, and there is that filantropic man that sacrifices his time and energy for a good cause, but treat his own family like shit. </div> </div> </div>Yep. And lots of Nephandi are this as well. Though usually on purpose and knowingly.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> This is how evil people are in real life, persons of light and shadows. </div> </div> </div>That's how most human evil goes. That's how a lot of people seen in the black and white views of history really were. But there are people guilty of being evil in ways where you can't point to as being shades like this. Explain the likes of Tommy Lynn Sells in your view of human evils. Where's his profit? What's convenient about his crimes? How was he not someone that admitted he was evil to himself? What good was he to.. anyone (besides a rare case for psychologists to study a serial killer). I can list lots of examples of evils you're just not addressing here.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I'll try to clarify this for once. I am not taking the responsability from the corrupted, by in the nephandic version, even if each one is to blame for his part, the all evil Nephandi are the final cause for all, so all the explanation ends in ultimate evil, instead of the more complex causes that we (or the character) could see, consider and discuss. </div> </div> </div>How are you not taking the responsibility from the corrupted if the Nephandi are the ultimate evil to the point where all complexity is removed.<br /> <br /> Lets look at Xerxes Jones. Lets say he wasn't actually corrupted (no indication in the books, though unhinged and suggestible) was given the idea to attack the Labyrinth to weaken the evils lurking at the edges of reality. There's still plenty of complexity to be had. There's how he got the spirit nuke. There's all the forces that helped him and hampered him. How did the spirit nuke get built. What was the investigation that went into his plan? Etc.<br /> <br /> You're not really establishing why the Nephandi being to blame kills complexity in all of this. It's not even like all the Nephandi are unified in their methods, efforts, or goals.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Still, when there aren't Nephandi to blame, characters can... </div> </div> </div>This only works if the PCs are all aware of it on its face.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The Technocracy is really, overwhelming powerful. But, did you stop to think what are the facing? </div> </div> </div>My point was what they're facing is the only reason why they aren't cartoonishly stupid.<br /> <br /> Without external opposition we're left with...the Technocrats being unable to organize themselves well enough to solve simple problems because their internal flaws are just that ridiculous.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> You see the same phenomenon and your explanation is &quot;Nephandi did this because they are evil&quot;. </div> </div> </div>I do? Where did I say that exactly?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Don't you see why I say that this ends with the moral dilemmas (and other kind of dilemmas)? </div> </div> </div>No, because you're just arguing in a circle and ignoring what I'm saying.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Yeah, but when one administer Euthanasia it gives the sick man a drug that ends his life peacefully and without pain, instead of throwing the pacient to a meat grinder. Nephandi are deliberately cruel. </div> </div> </div>Plenty of Nephandi are not portaryed as deliberately cruel in the books. At least from their internal perspectives.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> More so, when a doctor has to end the life of his pacient, he does it without pleasure, maybe even with tears in his face (I would cry if I had to do such a thing). Nephandi are described as sadistic, which conflicts with the moral excuses presented in the books. </div> </div> </div>Sadism isn't always as simple as being a Cenobite from Hellraiser.<br /> <br /> The target of your sadism doesn't have to be the person you kill, for example. A doctor could kill elderly women, using painless medications on those close to death anyway, and take sadistic pleasure in having them all &quot;change their wills&quot; to include him to thank him for his kindness, while screwing their families out of both their loved one and their inheritances (and I'm not actually making this up as something someone's done).<br /> <br /> So... how is that for a moral dilemma? One hand? An &quot;angel of mercy&quot; bringing painless death to the sick elderly. On the other hand? A con man murdering for profit with glee at the suffering his actions have on the families that don't even realize what happened.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> When you say &quot;my religious belief&quot; you are saying that my ideas, which I get from my experience and reasoning, are of the same nature of the ideas that some people get from cultural inheritance and faith. </div> </div> </div>No. We're saying that the beliefs are on the same topic, not of the same nature.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-03T09:29:24-07:00","id":538485,"title":"","content":"Why do the Nephandi have to be behind everything bad? What is the thinking with that? Is it to simplify matters down to one &quot;bad guy&quot; group?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-03T10:39:21-07:00","id":538531,"title":"","content":"Is the Nephandi infiltration of the Technocracy really that much of an issue for the metaplot of M20? It's completely ignoble, and unlike say the Avatar Storm of Revised it hasn't affected the game's actual mechanics or prevented players from easily playing a certain aspect of the game they enjoyed in past editions. Threat Null and the Technocracy Civil War are just as much supported ideas for the metaplot here as the Technephandi, and while you'll have to change the reason as to why the Disparate Alliance had to form that still doesn't impact the game in a drastic fashion that would require your Storyteller to painstakingly change things.<br /> <br /> So, yeah, not seeing the problem. The Golden Rule can easily be put in effect here.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1675-weirdboyz","name":"Weirdboyz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1675_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"212"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-03T16:26:54-07:00","id":538902,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Weirdboyz</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/528642-m20-technocracy-human-condition-vs-demonic-influence?p=538531#post538531\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Is the Nephandi infiltration of the Technocracy really that much of an issue for the metaplot of M20? It's completely ignoble, and unlike say the Avatar Storm of Revised it hasn't affected the game's actual mechanics or prevented players from easily playing a certain aspect of the game they enjoyed in past editions. Threat Null and the Technocracy Civil War are just as much supported ideas for the metaplot here as the Technephandi, and while you'll have to change the reason as to why the Disparate Alliance had to form that still doesn't impact the game in a drastic fashion that would require your Storyteller to painstakingly change things.<br /> <br /> So, yeah, not seeing the problem. The Golden Rule can easily be put in effect here.</div> </div> </div> </div>If you ask me, that Disparate Alliance is another mess. The essence of the Disparates was to be scattered, what's the point in doing Council of Traditions 2? And how about Crafts not in the Disparate Alliance? Will we have to invent a new faction of disparate Disparates? And why do we need the Telenephandi to move the Disparates? Just to make things easier from a philosophic point of view?<br /> <br /> Golden Rule rules, of course, but that does not keep me from being critic with the main line.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3673-mu_","name":"Mu_","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3673_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"99"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-08T17:51:41-07:00","id":546170,"title":"","content":"I too Am not fond of the &quot;Traditions V2 - Thothmes-Hermes, We're Mad At You&quot;. I'm annoyed with the nehpandi-as-Final-Boss-behind-everything, and am really starting to worry about the content in Technocracy Reloaded. <br /> <br /> While the nephandic infiltration is ignorable, I'd rather not have to carry around a 700 page book, of which I'm &quot;Ignoring&quot; about 200-300 pages of setting changes. I like the sidebars, but I really wish they were written in a slightly more neutral tone.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/55-necrotek","name":"necrotek","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar55_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"38"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-08T18:49:13-07:00","id":546285,"title":"","content":"As has been said, the ideas of a heavy nephandic infiltration aren't realy new, both old and new Convention Books heavily insinuated it. The only new thing it's to suspect of the inner Council itself, which should be dead or Threat Null-ed by now.<br /> <br /> Even then, as a plot development...it's lazy.<br /> <br /> Really, this is how I readed it in M20:<br /> -how can we justify those awful things like MECHA in our good-willed and heroic organization?<br /> -well, surely there were Nephandy behind all of that. It's not like the Tass recollected by MECHA was reason enough to maintain it for a non-Nephandi (not to count the HiTMarks that they built, or it's uses as maximun security prison).<br /> <br /> Actually, I like the idea of Technonephandy, and a heavy infiltrated Technocracy (that, again, isn't new), but I don't love the way they presented it. No hard feelings, though. <hr />\u200bAnd about the Traditions 2.0. In a hand, it makes easier to play Disparates. In another hand, it totally screws their allure as radicals that refused to give away any of their practices and beliefs to unify with the Traditions and survive.<br /> <br /> I think that the best would be to treat this aliance not as a way to have Taftany in the same room as Bata'a, but as a loose information and &quot;favour&quot; network between higly conflictive factions, that want to survive, but don't want any of the hippyism inherent in having a multitradition cabal, nor to comply to a common governmental power or a common law...<br /> <br /> That's it, I think they should be trated as even more conservative that the fabled OoH. For starters: No multidisparate cabal.<br /> Templars shouldn't come to the rescue of heathen Bata'a, but might provide information and resources against a common (and worse) enemy","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 06-08-2015, 09:54 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-08T21:45:11-07:00","id":546462,"title":"","content":"My main issue with saying that the Nephandi are behind it all, even moreso than \u201cthe devil made me do it\u201d serving as an all-too-convenient excuse, it that it undermines the Nephandi's distinctiveness. Yes, they've always been evil; but they've always been a particular <i>kind</i> of evil. I'm somewhat worried that <b>Book of the Fallen</b> will recast them in a way that causes them to lose their distinctiveness in the name of making them suitable for a broader set of villainous roles.<br /> <br /> All that said: I love there being <i>rumors of</i> Nephandic infiltration of the upper echelons of the Technocracy and/or the Traditions, with said rumors being the driving force behind the Disparate Alliance; but I also love the bit of advice from M20 that says \u201conce you've decided the extent of Nephandic corruption in your game, <i>don't tell the players</i>.\u201d","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":421,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","user":"Dataweaver","date":". 06-26-2017, 03:06 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-08T23:15:15-07:00","id":546568,"title":"","content":"I like that Evil (not corruption, because corruption requires something be pure/impure to begin with), can have many causes, not the least of which are Misinformation, Good Intentions, and &quot;I was too busy solving this other problem to notice my solution had horrible consequences elsewhere&quot;. The technocracy's higher echelons don't NEED nephandic corruption to be Batshit crazy, paranoid, delusional old men in encounter suits and exo-frames, directing the pogrom from the comfort of their Space Stations. Making them Nephandi weakens them - Instead of having their own motives,their own reasons, they just get the generic &quot;End it all&quot; motive. Instead of weighing the consequences of setting off nukes in a populated zone to kill Ravnos, they just hammer the &quot;Nuke it&quot; button. Nephandic control removes gravitas, removes humanity. The technocracy, even the crazy, ancient Arch-'Crats, needs to still have some humanity left. Even if it's just base, human pettiness.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/55-necrotek","name":"necrotek","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar55_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"38"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-08T23:35:43-07:00","id":546586,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ajax</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/528642-m20-technocracy-human-condition-vs-demonic-influence?p=538485#post538485\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Why do the Nephandi have to be behind everything bad? What is the thinking with that? Is it to simplify matters down to one &quot;bad guy&quot; group?</div> </div> </div> </div>No, it's about them being <i>damn good</i> at it. Especially if your World of Darkness is more than just Mage.<br /> <br /> Look, if you include Vampire and Werewolf alone, you have: 1) a conspiracy of ruthless immortal blood-drinkers with a lot of the same motivations as the Technocracy (i.e. convince the normals that there's nothing supernatural, and squish anything that might prove otherwise), 2) an <i>even worse</i> conspiracy of immortal blood drinkers that's sick of hiding and wants to turn the world into Vampire Hunter D, 3) a vast spiritual sickness slowly eating the world, 4) an army of hyper-corrupt regenerating super-soldiers, and 5) the world's biggest corporation that also happens to worship a universe-devouring horror that would make Cthulhu crap himself. How can the Nephandi <i>not</i> be doing well? 2-5 are their allies (obvious or otherwise), and 1 is an unwitting tool.<br /> <br /> Now, if I were using the Technocracy (I'm running a hybrid Ascension/Awakening game that borrows heavily from the Exarchs for its tyrants), I <i>wouldn't</i> have them directly controlled by the Nephandi. Heavily infiltrated? Yep! Lots of evil &quot;viziers&quot; whispering &quot;necessary evil&quot; justifications to the mages of Control, and the Syndicate would be theirs. The Traditions and DA wouldn't be any better off, though, with the Hermetics and Batini both as subverted as the Syndies, and the same &quot;let's you and him fight&quot; schtick across the board. And aside from which splats they might control outright, none of this should be a surprise. Of <i>course</i> the Nephandi are winning. It's been barely-hidden subtext from day one, the only difference being it's been upgraded to text. Fanaticism is their best friend, and every side of the Ascension War has plenty to spare.<br /> <br /> Frankly, I don't have much trouble swallowing the Technephandi subplot. I've been a Mage fan since 1e, and I have little problem with the notion that the Technocracy's dark side is really dark. However, IMO, it's the <i>idea</i> of the Technephandi that makes the best tool in the Fallen arsenal. It's a perfect half-truth to rekindle a war with, especially when you're selling the Tradinephandi across the road, and Dispanephandi the next town over. <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Monsters_Are_Due_on_Maple_Street\" target=\"_blank\">The Monsters are still due on Maple Street</a>, even to this day...<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Heroes die. Hope is a phoenix.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2434-captain-aeon","name":"Captain Aeon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2434_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"203"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-09T00:32:13-07:00","id":546624,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Captain Aeon</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/528642-m20-technocracy-human-condition-vs-demonic-influence?p=546586#post546586\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">No, it's about them being <i>damn good</i> at it. Especially if your World of Darkness is more than just Mage.<br /> <br /> Look, if you include Vampire and Werewolf alone, you have: 1) a conspiracy of ruthless immortal blood-drinkers with a lot of the same motivations as the Technocracy (i.e. convince the normals that there's nothing supernatural, and squish anything that might prove otherwise), 2) an <i>even worse</i> conspiracy of immortal blood drinkers that's sick of hiding and wants to turn the world into Vampire Hunter D, 3) a vast spiritual sickness slowly eating the world, 4) an army of hyper-corrupt regenerating super-soldiers, and 5) the world's biggest corporation that also happens to worship a universe-devouring horror that would make Cthulhu crap himself. How can the Nephandi <i>not</i> be doing well? 2-5 are their allies (obvious or otherwise), and 1 is an unwitting tool.<br /> <br /> Now, if I were using the Technocracy (I'm running a hybrid Ascension/Awakening game that borrows heavily from the Exarchs for its tyrants), I <i>wouldn't</i> have them directly controlled by the Nephandi. Heavily infiltrated? Yep! Lots of evil &quot;viziers&quot; whispering &quot;necessary evil&quot; justifications to the mages of Control, and the Syndicate would be theirs. The Traditions and DA wouldn't be any better off, though, with the Hermetics and Batini both as subverted as the Syndies, and the same &quot;let's you and him fight&quot; schtick across the board. And aside from which splats they might control outright, none of this should be a surprise. Of <i>course</i> the Nephandi are winning. It's been barely-hidden subtext from day one, the only difference being it's been upgraded to text. Fanaticism is their best friend, and every side of the Ascension War has plenty to spare.<br /> <br /> Frankly, I don't have much trouble swallowing the Technephandi subplot. I've been a Mage fan since 1e, and I have little problem with the notion that the Technocracy's dark side is really dark. However, IMO, it's the <i>idea</i> of the Technephandi that makes the best tool in the Fallen arsenal. It's a perfect half-truth to rekindle a war with, especially when you're selling the Tradinephandi across the road, and Dispanephandi the next town over. <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Monsters_Are_Due_on_Maple_Street\" target=\"_blank\">The Monsters are still due on Maple Street</a>, even to this day...</div> </div> </div> </div>And that's exactly what I don't like of the new approach: a world of good and evil (or evil and something in the middle) where every bad guy is controlled or allied with the evil evils.<br /> <br /> I don't know what the Camarilla has to do with Nephandi. Neither the Sabbat. I remind you that the Sabbat has an Inquisition that fights the demonic influence, and that they fight to stop the end of the world. Of course they are evil to humans, but they don't fit in your axis of good and evil.<br /> <br /> About Werewolf, I won't discuss that. First, because that game was the first to have the evil evils as main theme, a force behind every evil in the world, and that's one of the two reasons I don't like the game at all. Second, because that's precisely what they are turning Mage into, a world where the Wyrm is the force behind all evil. Having a neutral Mage setting, with the Technocracy not being cartoonishly evil, took a lot of effort, but it arrived with GttT and Revised Edition. This is a step back.<br /> <br /> In my world, you don't need to have a Nephandi whispering &quot;necessary&quot; evil to the Technocracy, because it's war, because it's human nature, and even you can think that because is the ultimate conclusion of their philosophy (others Techies could not agree, and then we have conflict and dilemmas). Again, if Tech 1 kidnaps a few humans to experiment with them to find a cure to a mortal disease, Tech 2 can disagree and be sick of it. In my scenario, there is a moral dilemma there. In yours, there is a Nephandi whispering to Tech 1, then you can know that Tech 1 is doing evil, end of the dilemma.<br /> <br /> In my world, I don't need the Nephandi everywhere whispering to everyone to have them fighting and getting the wose of everyone, it's war, it's human nature, and maybe they are the ultimate conlusions of most of their philosophies.<br /> <br /> At last, some of you have said that all that has always been the subtext of the game. Some days ago I remembered one episode, where Ravnos Antediluvian woke and it could have been the end of the world. The Technocracy elevated the situation to code Ragnarok, which means that they have to stop the menace in any posible way without regard of the consecuences. Any number of civil casuaties were allowed, but also any quantity of Technocracy resources and even any number of Technocrat casualty (this could mean 100%). Of course, that is logical, if something can mean the end of the world, it make sense that no sacrifice is too much, since there would be nothing if the fail. At the end of the day, all was very Technocrat, the menace stopped, a lot of civil casualties, radiation that the Bangkok people will have to suffer, and a campaign to clean evidences and brainwash everyone. But they saved the world. And that's the point, the Techncoracy can be the bad guys and they are, but maybe the world could not exist without them (and that &quot;maybe&quot; is another dilemma).<br /> <br /> If they were Nephandi, they could have easily destroyed the world, by letting Gehenna happen, or by taking advantage of the chaotic situation and dropping those nukes everywhere. The Inner Circle could have sold to the other Technocrats that there were other menaces in the main cities.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3673-mu_","name":"Mu_","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3673_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"99"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-09T06:01:42-07:00","id":546842,"title":"","content":"I've never had a problem with Werewolf, possibly because I like a good vs evil storyline, but more likely because the Ultimate Corrupter is itself a corruption of something more pure. Likewise I don't have a problem with the Nephandi being evil. For at least 15 years now I've considered them to be the closest thing to pure evil in the cWoD, because unlike all the others they <i>had</i> to choose it. What I do have a problem is with them somehow being much less fallible than anyone else; better at hiding themselves, better at manipulation, better at either planning or recruitment, and just better.<br /> <br /> Now, don't get me wrong, they should seem to be that dark force that (seems to have) realized the secret Truth (that there is no Truth, just Lies) and is stronger because of it. That's the most terrifying thing about them, that they could be <i>right</i>. They play well as an implacable, overwhelming, incorruptible evil that is winning despite its obvious flaws. It's a great story, one that clearly fits in Mage.<br /> <br /> They just can't be objectively right.<br /> <br /> For starters, there's nothing about the Nephandi, mechanically, that makes them particularly special. They get two advantages no one else does. Because of the Cauls, they (by default) can't be infiltrated effectively and they can't be redeemed. But note, that doesn't actually make them any better at infiltrating or manipulating people. Sure, there are no double agents among the Nephandi who might be wrongly trusted with the NOC list (I just watched the first Mission Impossible movie, sue me), but they can still be spied on and they can still turn on each other. And at least an Etherite among the Technocrats doesn't have to hide a corrupted Avatar. So the idea that they are <i>the</i> force secretly manipulating everyone and <i>not</i> being manipulated in return, doesn't really make sense. They can be, but its not fait accompli. <br /> <br /> Also, the history of the Technocracy and the Traditions has always been problematic with me. It is riddled with bad choices and misunderstandings, which is fine and appropriate, but too often their history falls into the pattern of a Parade of Failures which relies too much on people being blinded by arrogance and flaws at inappropriate times. Yeah, its in keeping with the cWoD and magely hubris, and its often necessary to get everyone into the position they are in today, but that doesn't keep it from being clunky (and sometimes a little stupid). But if it has to happen, it should be happening to the Nephandi too, they are as prone to hubris and self delusion as any Mage. If they are one of the Big Boys and Girls they should be fucking up like them too.<br /> <br /> Because then, if an ST wants to have the Nephandi be that &quot;implacable, overwhelming, incorruptible evil that is winning despite its obvious flaws,&quot; they have the obvious flaws to win despite.<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-09T08:08:55-07:00","id":547070,"title":"","content":"Well, that's a classic problem with Nephandi. There isn't examples or metaplot explaining just how and why are they so damn good at infiltrating. Older books depict most nephandy practices and magick as really un-subtle. Like eating babies and such. If the baby eater nephandi are just a bait, then who are the ones that aren't a bait?<br /> <br /> However, I think that M20 it's better at portraying this aspect of Nephandi than was, say, the Book of Madness. With things like the technonephandi, and the infiltrated corporation of christian mercs, one can <i>almost </i>believe that they can be good infiltrators. I hope more subtlety for the Nephandi for their future book.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-09T08:37:13-07:00","id":547109,"title":"","content":"I prefer to run my Ascension games as having the Union's faults being entirely human, for a number of reasons - first, I don't like crossover with Werewolf, and i don't like Werewolf's metaphysics intruding on Mage. Secondly, having everything be the Nephandi's fault let's the Technocratic Union off the hook for the destruction of Earth's biosphere and the absolute and utter failure of western capitalism. The Union could give the world a post scarcity paradise, but instead, because they enjoy power and don't want to share, the Union has chosen to give the Sleepers a World of Darkness. I run a leftist, political World of Darkness game, and I don't let their be a ooga booga inhuman monster to blame the world's problems on. The reason why the world is dying in the WOD lies squarely at the feet of the Technocratic Union, who has failed in dealing with the vampire problem, the climate change crisis, and the cultural addiction to global capitalism. I've never seen a poor Technocrat. Also, bad guy Technocrats are *cool* - if I wanted a benevolent Technocracy, I'd run the United Federation of Planets from Star Trek. Instead, I find Agent Smith from The Matrix and the Village from The Prisoner much more compelling images and themes.<br /> <br /> I push the failures of western culture straight into the faces of my players, and I don't white wash it at all. I challenge my players to find solutions - solutions which the 9 Traditions, thrown through the crucible, cultural themes and tools now in the hands of ordinary, working people, no longer the property of elites - can use.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6358-robert-armsley","name":"Robert Armsley","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6358_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"72"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-09T08:58:37-07:00","id":547118,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Robert Armsley</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/528642-m20-technocracy-human-condition-vs-demonic-influence?p=547109#post547109\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I prefer to run my Ascension games as having the Union's faults being entirely human, for a number of reasons - first, I don't like crossover with Werewolf, and i don't like Werewolf's metaphysics intruding on Mage. Secondly, having everything be the Nephandi's fault let's the Technocratic Union off the hook for the destruction of Earth's biosphere and the absolute and utter failure of western capitalism.</div> </div> </div> </div>The crossover aspect is largely irrelevant to the idea of the Nephandi. While the books have long since had Wyrm/Nephandus crossover, there are plenty of options with the Nephandi that could care less about that. They stand as their own thing.<br /> <br /> I also continue to not get the whole excluded middle thing of, &quot;the Nephandi infiltrating the Union removes all human faults and all Union guilty.&quot;<br /> <br /> 1) People with power being corrupted by bad people is a human fault. It happens all the time in the real world. No Technocrats ever getting corrupted would demonstrate a lack of human failings, and corrupting elements generally target the most powerful members of an organization they can sink their teeth into. If the mob wants to bribe a new anti-crime bill out of existence in their state, it's a lot easier to sabotage by getting specific powerful individuals (committee chairs, speakers/etc. in the general assembly, the presiding executive with veto power) than trying to actually corrupt 51+% of the legislature. If humans in the real world perpetuate both sides of that, why should mages (with the goal of having human faults matter to mages) be immune to the same pressures?<br /> <br /> 2) Why does being corrupted by an evil group absolve you of guilt for their actions? If the mob can only smuggle in a shipment of guns because a politician signed some paperwork for them, that politician is culpable for every person harmed with those guns (and probably whatever bad things the mob does with the money from selling the guns if they're doing that too). &quot;I just signed some papers for a bribe!&quot; doesn't make you morally clean in all this. Likewise, if a Technocrat makes a deal with a Nephandus to sabotage their rival Technocrat's research in exchange for access to a Technocratic database, that Technocrat is still morally culpable for when the Nephandus uses that intel to blackmail a someone into a much more dubious action.<br /> <br /> I simply don't get this incredibly black &amp; white view of morality that follows the idea that being corrupted makes you squeaky clean in the end.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-09T09:23:01-07:00","id":547141,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Robert Armsley</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/528642-m20-technocracy-human-condition-vs-demonic-influence?p=547109#post547109\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I prefer to run my Ascension games as having the Union's faults being entirely human, for a number of reasons - first, I don't like crossover with Werewolf, and i don't like Werewolf's metaphysics intruding on Mage. Secondly, having everything be the Nephandi's fault let's the Technocratic Union off the hook for the destruction of Earth's biosphere and the absolute and utter failure of western capitalism. The Union could give the world a post scarcity paradise, but instead, because they enjoy power and don't want to share, the Union has chosen to give the Sleepers a World of Darkness. I run a leftist, political World of Darkness game, and I don't let their be a ooga booga inhuman monster to blame the world's problems on. The reason why the world is dying in the WOD lies squarely at the feet of the Technocratic Union, who has failed in dealing with the vampire problem, the climate change crisis, and the cultural addiction to global capitalism. I've never seen a poor Technocrat. Also, bad guy Technocrats are *cool* - if I wanted a benevolent Technocracy, I'd run the United Federation of Planets from Star Trek. Instead, I find Agent Smith from The Matrix and the Village from The Prisoner much more compelling images and themes.<br /> <br /> I push the failures of western culture straight into the faces of my players, and I don't white wash it at all. I challenge my players to find solutions - solutions which the 9 Traditions, thrown through the crucible, cultural themes and tools now in the hands of ordinary, working people, no longer the property of elites - can use.</div> </div> </div> </div>Personally, I would make the Craftmasons, who went into hiding before the Lighting Purge of the 1800's as a Disparate, become these egalitarian socialist types. Still believing in making the world a better place for man, they're the most egalitarian of all the factions, Technocracy, Traditions, or Disparate alike. They promoted the worker communes of pre-civil war Spain before they flee to Scandinavia and develop the area into what they consider a near-perfect bastion that still needs to have the kinks worked out. They're against the Syndicate for obvious reasons, and against the NWO for being both totalitarian and giving true socialism a tarnished reputation. They find the Traditions being stuck up elitists who thinks they know what's right for the people. Hence why they decide to join the DA.<br /> <br /> The current Craftmasons are a secret society of both aware Sleepers and Awaken. The nine guilds are reformed into NGOs, trade unions, and community militia. They're an electronic democracy using hidden networks to prevent hackers from finding them. And the current leader and a sort-of foreign affairs officer, the Representative, is elected into the position and is an aware Sleeper.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6163-yama951","name":"Yama951","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"33"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-09T10:56:43-07:00","id":547315,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/528642-m20-technocracy-human-condition-vs-demonic-influence?p=547118#post547118\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I also continue to not get the whole excluded middle thing of, &quot;the Nephandi infiltrating the Union removes all human faults and all Union guilty.&quot;</div> </div> </div> </div>I dunno, I kinda get it. I mean, &quot;susceptible to Nephandic seduction&quot; is a purely understandable human fault, but there's a danger of that robbing a critique of teeth. If the Union's leadership is heavily undermined by Nephandic conversion (&quot;many members are barabbi or were never Technocrats to begin with&quot;) - which is deeper than merely self-serving corruption but involves a devoted commitment to a nihilistic theology - then the Union ceases to be a meaningful critique of the way in which impersonal and dehumanizing systems can grind people into putty, and becomes in essence an argument that such an institution is a problem only because it was staffed with human people who can be undermined by corrupt theology; that the Union is only troublesome because it invests humans with power and power attracts corruption.<br /> <br /> I think the Union is more <i>useful </i>from a storytelling perspective if, when it is discovered that they are doing something awful, it is because their organizational ideology and objectives have led to this consequence, rather than a story about how the well-meaning field agents have been conned into doing something awful by infiltrators that actually are insincere about their ideology and have the infliction of awful consequences <i>as an actual objective</i>. While &quot;being conned into uncritically serving leaders who turn out to be corrupt&quot; is also a form of guilt, it's not a terribly <i>interesting </i>one for the Union.<br /> <br /> Perversely, it seems that Nephandic infiltration results in the story arguing <i>in accord</i> with the Union's ideology: The problem isn't the System, it's that the System is composed of humans. The only cure would be systems devoid of humanity.<br /> <br /> I would compare it to some of the worst presentations of Pentex as merely a money-hemorrhaging death cult. That Pentex does not and cannot serve as a meaningful critique of corporate practices because it does not say &quot;Those motivated solely by rapacious greed will crush the world underfoot to secure it&quot; but &quot;those motivated by rapacious greed may be vulnerable to corruption that makes them abandon that greed in favor of crushing the world underfoot due to Wyrmish mind gribblies.&quot;<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Yama951</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/528642-m20-technocracy-human-condition-vs-demonic-influence?p=547141#post547141\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Personally, I would make the Craftmasons...</div> </div> </div> </div>I know it's probably unsatisfying to people that loved Sorcerer's Crusade, but to my mind the modern Craftmasons are either Utopian conspirators in the Union or SoE/VA members. The alternative seems to be that the SoE's Communist utopianism and the VA's association with anarchism and communalism are insincere or inadequate, because the sainted Craftmaons are the only ones that really care about the common man.<br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i> I attack people with giant insects both </i>on <i>and </i>off <i>the court.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1653-five-eyes","name":"Five Eyes","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1653_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"395"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-09T11:50:22-07:00","id":547382,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Five Eyes</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/528642-m20-technocracy-human-condition-vs-demonic-influence?p=547315#post547315\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I dunno, I kinda get it. I mean, &quot;susceptible to Nephandic seduction&quot; is a purely understandable human fault, but there's a danger of that robbing a critique of teeth. If the Union's leadership is heavily undermined by Nephandic conversion (&quot;many members are barabbi or were never Technocrats to begin with&quot;) - which is deeper than merely self-serving corruption but involves a devoted commitment to a nihilistic theology - then the Union ceases to be a meaningful critique of the way in which impersonal and dehumanizing systems can grind people into putty, and becomes in essence an argument that such an institution is a problem only because it was staffed with human people who can be undermined by corrupt theology; that the Union is only troublesome because it invests humans with power and power attracts corruption.<br /> <br /> I think the Union is more <i>useful </i>from a storytelling perspective if, when it is discovered that they are doing something awful, it is because their organizational ideology and objectives have led to this consequence, rather than a story about how the well-meaning field agents have been conned into doing something awful by infiltrators that actually are insincere about their ideology and have the infliction of awful consequences <i>as an actual objective</i>. While &quot;being conned into uncritically serving leaders who turn out to be corrupt&quot; is also a form of guilt, it's not a terribly <i>interesting </i>one for the Union.</div> </div> </div> </div>Indeed, there are many varieties of corruption and the Technocracy lends itself to several that the Nephandi do not. Most notably Noble Cause Corruption, being unethical in the pursuit of a higher cause. That's practically the Technocracy's bread and butter. The Fallen can facilitate that kind of corruption with influence, whispers, and opportunity, but they severely impair it if they have too much control.<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-09T12:54:13-07:00","id":547447,"title":"","content":"Okay, I'm going to try to sum up: most of the complaints about the Nephandi winning seem to boil down to: &quot;can't the failings of the Technocracy/Traditions/whoever be just <i>human</i> failings?&quot;<br /> <br /> Yep. They are. And that's exactly <i>why</i> the Nephandi are winning.<br /> <br /> They're not creating any of those problems. They're just <i>exacerbating</i> them. Nephandi nudge, they prod, they make the floor just that bit more slick for other mages to stumble and fall, but all they do is change the TN. The Path is Fragile, and the metaphysical gravity of hubris, terror and fanaticism combine to make Descent so much easier than Ascension. Transcendence is tricky and rare; atrocity is never more than one weakness away. Revenge is simpler than forgiveness. And, frankly, there are plenty of folks who don't need Nephandi to be unspeakably evil. Look at Voormas. He's explicitly <i>not</i> a Nephandus, but a lot of Nephandi probably wish they were half as terrifying as he is. The world is already an awful, tangled mess. Add Nephandi, and how hard do they even need to work? They just cut off the occasional genuine reformer. A saint becomes a martyr here, a hero becomes a dead legend there, and then you make sure the other side takes the blame.<br /> <br /> Most importantly, the Nephandi <i>aren't</i> infallible. If they were, it wouldn't be much of a campaign. They're really good at what they do, but after centuries of their foul efforts, the world is still here. The Technocracy and Traditions <i>did</i> get over their mutual hatred for long enough to stop the Aswadim back in the 40s. If the right group were to make the right efforts, who knows? The Awakened of the world will never stop arguing, but maybe a shared enemy and a few moments of clarity can get them to stop <i>killing</i> each other. It'd be a lot of work, and the Nephandi resist nothing more than peace. Now give a group of PCs the right motive to try it, and it could make one Hell of a campaign, couldn't it? <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/cool.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Cool\" smilieid=\"6\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Heroes die. Hope is a phoenix.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2434-captain-aeon","name":"Captain Aeon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2434_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"203"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-09T14:00:32-07:00","id":547490,"title":"","content":"I think most people isn't complaining with the Nephandi winning the Ascension War. We're complaining about Nephandi being, if not in charge, at least in a position of comand inside the Technocracy.<br /> <br /> The fact that Nepandi want to exacerbate the human failings of the Technos wouldn't be so invasive if they weren't giving the orders (or heavily influencing them).<br /> <br /> Since the excesses of the Technocracy are being exacerbated due to Nephandi infiltration. Then, at least a good portion of the wrongdoings can be blamed to bad advice/command, instead on an imperialist philosophy, or normal (non exacerbated) human failings.<br /> <br /> The Inner Circle left MECHA to be, long after Tech-guys noted it was a perversion of their ideals. This may be due to sincere greed (or other human failings), but since the Inner Circle (or a good portion of it) are Nephandi, then one can argue that if weren't for those Nephandi, then MECHA wouldn't have existed that long. This inefably takes a good portion of the blame outside mere Technocratic hubris. So, the main problem it's not Nephandi infiltration (exept for those that just don't like Nephandi), but that they seem to be &quot;in charge&quot; of the operation (or very well positioned, at least).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-09T15:03:14-07:00","id":547577,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/528642-m20-technocracy-human-condition-vs-demonic-influence?p=547490#post547490\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think most people isn't complaining with the Nephandi winning the Ascension War. We're complaining about Nephandi being, if not in charge, at least in a position of comand inside the Technocracy.</div> </div> </div> </div>This. To me, the most compelling narrative is the one where the protagonists finally confront the Fallen who they think has been pulling everyones' strings, and she sits back and laughs, saying \u201cyou think <i>I'm</i> responsible for all of this? That's the beauty of it; you did this to yourselves! Sure, I gave a nudge here and there; but those would have been completely ineffectual if you people hadn't already been leaning that way to begin with!<br /> <br /> \u201cWhy would I <i>want</i> to usurp control of your leaders, when they're running the world into the ground so effectively <i>on their own?</i>\u201d","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":421,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","user":"Dataweaver","date":". 07-31-2020, 11:35 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-09T15:13:43-07:00","id":547586,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/528642-m20-technocracy-human-condition-vs-demonic-influence?p=547490#post547490\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">We're complaining about Nephandi being, if not in charge, at least in a position of comand inside the Technocracy.</div> </div> </div> </div>Then don't use that Future Fate option?<br /> <br /> I mean, the whole point of the Future Fate things, even for ones where there is a clearly stated bias/default that M20 goes with, is that they open up play to multiple official variations. How bad the Nephandi influence on the Technocracy is has more than one option in M20.<br /> <br /> Part of the issue seems like taking the first listed Future Fate as absolute unchangeable fact in the setting. It was where the Mage metaplot was headed, but other options are presented so that the Technocracy can be styled to what best suits your game.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-09T16:56:35-07:00","id":547736,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/528642-m20-technocracy-human-condition-vs-demonic-influence?p=547577#post547577\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">This. To me, the most compelling narrative is the one where the protagonists finally confront the Fallen who they think has been pulling everyones' strings, and she sits back and laughs, saying \u201cyou think <i>I'm</i> responsible for all of this? That's the beauty of it; you did this to yourselves! Sure, I gave a nudge here and there; but those would have been completely ineffectual if your people hadn't already been leaning that way to begin with!<br /> <br /> \u201cWhy would I <i>want</i> to usurp control of your leaders, when they're running the world into the ground so effectively <i>on their own?</i>\u201d</div> </div> </div> </div>Sounds like we're on the same endgame page, at least. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> And I'm really hearing Jodi Blake there. (Yes, that's a compliment, scary as it may sound.)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Heroes die. Hope is a phoenix.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2434-captain-aeon","name":"Captain Aeon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2434_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"203"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-10T03:02:57-07:00","id":548191,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/528642-m20-technocracy-human-condition-vs-demonic-influence?p=547586#post547586\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Then don't use that Future Fate option?<br /> <br /> I mean, the whole point of the Future Fate things, even for ones where there is a clearly stated bias/default that M20 goes with, is that they open up play to multiple official variations. How bad the Nephandi influence on the Technocracy is has more than one option in M20.<br /> <br /> Part of the issue seems like taking the first listed Future Fate as absolute unchangeable fact in the setting. It was where the Mage metaplot was headed, but other options are presented so that the Technocracy can be styled to what best suits your game.</div> </div> </div> </div>It's not presented as a future fate. It's presented as the Technephandi being in power for AGES. The charm of the Technocracy is in the fact that they are people with good goals and ideas who are doing horrible things for the greater good. The part of their story and joy in playing them is deciding if it needs reform or if they are even right! If the Technocracy has been a Nephandi pawn all along, they lose their agency, and make plots about figuring out the morality of the Technocracy moot.<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:arial\">What doesn't kill you, makes you...<i> stranger</i>.</span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1182-asmodai","name":"Asmodai","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1182_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2989"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-10T03:39:55-07:00","id":548227,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/528642-m20-technocracy-human-condition-vs-demonic-influence?p=547118#post547118\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I also continue to not get the whole excluded middle thing of, &quot;the Nephandi infiltrating the Union removes all human faults and all Union guilty.&quot;</div> </div> </div> </div>I got your point: &quot;the Nephandi do not corrupt people, they have to be bad before they can be corrupted&quot;.<br /> <br /> I could accept it, but even if that didn't take responsibilities from the corrupts, the goals of the corrupts are those of the Nephandi. The corrupted politician from your example is evil, but he isn't doing the bad things by himself, but he is following the agenda of his corrupter. When a politician cuts back social budgets he is doing so to advance the Nephandi agenda (this example is taken directly from M20).<br /> <br /> Going back from my example in USSR. The communist party and the people in it aimed to make a more just and free society for all. And while a lot of people improved their economic status, basic rigths as free speech were denied to people, they were subjects of strong propaganda and disidents were put in terrible camps of reeducation. The repression level in Stalin times was hellish, and in all USSR history the people was opressed by those who were ment to free them. The supposedly egalitarian society was dominated by a bureaucratic elite. How is that the movement created to better the situation of the people ended like this?<br /> <br /> M20 response is that there were some devil worshippers who tainted or bribed the people in charge, just to end the world, or at least to make it a worse place. Those bureacrats still needed to be corruptable, but they followed the evil dudes agenda.<br /> <br /> Truth is, of course, much more complicated, when you remove the demonic influence and you have to look to human causes. And that way is how I like it, because it removes the simple answer. In the nephandic version, USSR is evil because is the product of the devil worshipers. In real world, it has its lights and its shadows, and, while it was a terrible institution, we could look back and try to figure where the path was twisted, and (suppose we are in the 80s) try to figure how could be fixed or if it has to be destroyed. And to fix it would be way more complicated that to find and destroy those satanic dudes.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Captain Aeon</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">And, frankly, there are plenty of folks who don't need Nephandi to be unspeakably evil.</div> </div> </div> </div>Exactly.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Look at Voormas. He's explicitly <i>not</i> a Nephandus, but a lot of Nephandi probably wish they were half as terrifying as he is. </div> </div> </div>Yeah. I just have read about him, and he is a villain with credible motivations.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3673-mu_","name":"Mu_","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3673_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"99"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-10T09:16:26-07:00","id":548788,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Asmodai</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/528642-m20-technocracy-human-condition-vs-demonic-influence?p=548191#post548191\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It's not presented as a future fate.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yes. It is. Or at least I can't find anything outside of the Future Fate boxes that actually treats this as undeniable truth. The Disparate Alliance largely believes it to be the case, but the book calls them out as a belief, not a truth they have sole absolute knowledge of. The text very clearly presents the DA's belief that the Techphandi are real as a unifying core to their unity. The group is heavily built around long time dedicated anti-Nephandi, or anti-Technocracy, Crafts that got convinced to join together under the claim that their enemies are one and the same; and they can't survive without some what to stop this.<br /> <br /> The Technocracy and Nephandi sections of the book generally do not get phrased as starkly as you're saying outside of Future Fate boxes as options. Though its a 700 page book, if I missed something, please cite.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> If the Technocracy has been a Nephandi pawn all along, they lose their agency, and make plots about figuring out the morality of the Technocracy moot. </div> </div> </div>Except the Nephandi don't rob people of agency. The Techpandi success doesn't stop the Technocratic civil war being a thing, where the idealist Technocrats try to root out the corruption that's ruined their Union; and still have to struggle with how to keep themselves clean of corruption since the Union's methods are part of what made them so easily corrupted in the first place.<br /> <br /> It's still a gigantic excluded middle. The only two options you're willing to consider (despite the book presenting lots of dials on this) are, &quot;The Technocracy isn't that corrupted by the Nephandi enough to matter so all their faults are their own,&quot; or, &quot;Complete pawns with no free will or agency and everything is the Nehpandi conspiracy.&quot; That's not what the book says are the options at hand.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Mu_</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/528642-m20-technocracy-human-condition-vs-demonic-influence?p=548227#post548227\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I got your point: &quot;the Nephandi do not corrupt people, they have to be bad before they can be corrupted&quot;.</div> </div> </div> </div>So, no, you don't get my point at all.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I could accept it, but even if that didn't take responsibilities from the corrupts, the goals of the corrupts are those of the Nephandi. The corrupted politician from your example is evil, but he isn't doing the bad things by himself, but he is following the agenda of his corrupter. When a politician cuts back social budgets he is doing so to advance the Nephandi agenda (this example is taken directly from M20). </div> </div> </div>You might want to actually reread the book on how the Nephandi operate. Because they most certainly don't have access to an automatic 1000 success mind control power that strips anyone that makes a deal with them of all free will, agency, personal agenda, or morality. But that's consistently how you treat them.<br /> <br /> The politician in my example is doing bad things (whether he knows it or not). He's doing them because they follow his agenda. The Nephandi take advantage of these things. They look for places (like all corrupting forces) where the agendas of bad people have cooperative goals with their own agenda. The politician's agenda needs money and wants to look tough on crime, so he takes bribes from one criminal organization and they feed him their rivals to throw in jail; all the while that organization grows more power with less rivals and a politician doing favors for them.<br /> <br /> Corrupting people takes making them at least think that their goals are compatible with your goals. They don't just get you to cut back on social programs to make life worse for the poor out of the blue. They take advantage of the current political climate of austerity, and distrust in government, to offer politicians safety from the political repercussions of tossing millions on their asses by removing government assistance.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> M20 response is that there were some devil worshippers who tainted or bribed the people in charge, just to end the world, or at least to make it a worse place. Those bureacrats still needed to be corruptable, but they followed the evil dudes agenda. </div> </div> </div>Few Nephandi are devil worshipers.<br /> <br /> And M20's response is a lot of things. The USSR, in M20, is like most governments: a victim of the Ascension War. It ended the way it did not just because of the Nephandi, but because humans with god-like power are running around using that power. Mage conflicts intersect with Sleeper conflicts, and generally things get worse (esp. with the Nephandi around to push things towards being worse).<br /> <br /> ----<br /> <br /> Listen, Mu_, I get it. You don't care to see the Nephandi as anything other than cartoonish evil, you don't care what the books actually say about them, and you want to run the games the way you see them. But there's not point in all of this if you can't at least be respectful of the fact that you're personal view on them are not only not universal <b>but not what's in the books</b><br /> <br /> You've tried to brush this off in the past, but we keep getting to the same exact point. You think the Nephandi are stupid, and you think everything the game lets them touch is stupid for it. That's all you're managing to say","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-10T10:02:10-07:00","id":548860,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/528642-m20-technocracy-human-condition-vs-demonic-influence?p=548788#post548788\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Yes. It is. Or at least I can't find anything outside of the Future Fate boxes that actually treats this as undeniable truth. The Disparate Alliance largely believes it to be the case, but the book calls them out as a belief, not a truth they have sole absolute knowledge of. The text very clearly presents the DA's belief that the Techphandi are real as a unifying core to their unity. The group is heavily built around long time dedicated anti-Nephandi, or anti-Technocracy, Crafts that got convinced to join together under the claim that their enemies are one and the same; and they can't survive without some what to stop this.<br /> <br /> The Technocracy and Nephandi sections of the book generally do not get phrased as starkly as you're saying outside of Future Fate boxes as options. Though its a 700 page book, if I missed something, please cite.</div> </div> </div> </div>At several points of the book, it makes reference to the Technocracy as a tool of the Nephandi (I recall mainly the Nephandi and Disparate chapters). It is true that it could be subjective perspective (although no narrator is presented) but also that it is a theme traversing the entire book, too present to ignore.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/528642-m20-technocracy-human-condition-vs-demonic-influence?p=548788#post548788\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Except the Nephandi don't rob people of agency. The Techpandi success doesn't stop the Technocratic civil war being a thing, where the idealist Technocrats try to root out the corruption that's ruined their Union; and still have to struggle with how to keep themselves clean of corruption since the Union's methods are part of what made them so easily corrupted in the first place.</div> </div> </div> </div>To start with, in the nephandi-free Technocracy the civil war isn't mandatory. As an idealist Technocrat you could agree with the means (the reality is in such danger that extreme measures are in order. you could disagree but conform, you could try to make your work decently and use the space you have to make things right (as John Courage did with Lee Ann), you could struggle for power in the usual channels, you could sabotage evil Technocrat or you could do a lot of things before starting a war that could be devastating for the Technocracy and even the Masses. Of course, you could go for a war, it's your choice, but there are a lot of posible positions.<br /> <br /> If Nephandi run the Inner Circle or have a great control of the Technocracy, few solutions remain aside from war.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/528642-m20-technocracy-human-condition-vs-demonic-influence?p=548788#post548788\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Few Nephandi are devil worshipers.</div> </div> </div> </div>I know, but I was trying to make an example in a neutral setting. If they worship the Void or the Wyrm, they want the destruction of the world or are actual devil worshipers is not the point in the example.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/528642-m20-technocracy-human-condition-vs-demonic-influence?p=548788#post548788\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">And M20's response is a lot of things. The USSR, in M20, is like most governments: a victim of the Ascension War. It ended the way it did not just because of the Nephandi, but because humans with god-like power are running around using that power. Mage conflicts intersect with Sleeper conflicts, and generally things get worse (esp. with the Nephandi around to push things towards being worse).</div> </div> </div> </div>Again, I was not talking of the URSS in Mage: the Ascension universe. It was a an example of how to explain the reality in worlds with or without forces of absolute evil. Here, the USSR is a metaphor of the Technocracy, and how noble goals can be subverted by the ones carrying them. An example that I find quite precise, despite the Syndicate. I would ask to please reread the example with that in mind.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/528642-m20-technocracy-human-condition-vs-demonic-influence?p=548788#post548788\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Listen, Mu_, I get it. You don't care to see the Nephandi as anything other than cartoonish evil, you don't care what the books actually say about them, and you want to run the games the way you see them. But there's not point in all of this if you can't at least be respectful of the fact that you're personal view on them are not only not universal <b>but not what's in the books</b><br /> <br /> You've tried to brush this off in the past, but we keep getting to the same exact point. You think the Nephandi are stupid, and you think everything the game lets them touch is stupid for it. That's all you're managing to say</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't know what you understand by &quot;cartoonish evil&quot;. For me, they are those bad guys who appeared in cartoons, some of them cheesy, some of them very appealing, who wanted to detroy the world or do evil things only because they the bad guys.<br /> <br /> We tried before to analize the justifications of the Nephandi. I told you that did not make sense, and you said that made sense but was not logical. Anyway, then they are stupid (sorry if I misunderstood you, I confess I am not sure of what do you mean with that).<br /> <br /> That is because they have no real motivations. They were invented as pure evil, and then a set of artificial excuses have been assembled to justify their existence and their actions, but they are not consistent.<br /> <br /> I've played the Sabbat, and with them I have played diabolically evil characters who killed and tortured without a whim, and I enjoyed it. But when I played a Nephandus I ended being more good than I should because I could not really see the point.<br /> <br /> Anyway, I am not against the Nephandi, I think that a Nephandus or two can be useful as plot artifacts. But what I cannot abide is to surrender the entire setting to those kind of absolute evil.<br /> <br /> And yes, I have read the Nephandi chapter. Two times actually. Still not convinced.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3673-mu_","name":"Mu_","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3673_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"99"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-10T12:41:53-07:00","id":549186,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/528642-m20-technocracy-human-condition-vs-demonic-influence?p=548788#post548788\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It's still a gigantic excluded middle. The only two options you're willing to consider (despite the book presenting lots of dials on this) are, &quot;The Technocracy isn't that corrupted by the Nephandi enough to matter so all their faults are their own,&quot; or, &quot;Complete pawns with no free will or agency and everything is the Nehpandi conspiracy.&quot; That's not what the book says are the options at hand.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think it's less excluded middle and more disagreement on what the middle is. The situation you present as middle ground, that the Nephandi have infiltrated enough to whisper dark offers and set up tragic choices, seems to fall firmly on the 'not corrupted enough to matter' side. So it sometimes seems like you are hair splitting, or others are excluding the middle, when in fact you are both just starting from different points.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> You might want to actually reread the book on how the Nephandi operate. Because they most certainly don't have access to an automatic 1000 success mind control power that strips anyone that makes a deal with them of all free will, agency, personal agenda, or morality. But that's consistently how you treat them. </div> </div> </div>No, indeed, but I think that's closer to the actual problem. We have no idea what the actual success rate of Nephandic plots is. We get quite a bit about the Technocracy and the Traditions setting up their own problems, ignoring potential threats, and generally screwing up due to their own preconceptions and views. There's very little of that kind of history among the Nephandi. Now I realize that's actually because the Nephandi are an ST toolkit to tailor, not a playable faction, but combined with the idea that the Nephandi are a constantly growing threat it gives the impression that they are somehow much better at recruitment, lying, planning, coordination, manipulation, and pretty much everything. There's this feeling they've been given all the cards without a good reason why.<br /> <br /> Now the Nephandi chapter does address this somewhat by talking about how its not that they don't get caught, its that they are good at pinning the blame on other groups, but that only somewhat mitigates the problem for me. <br /> <br /> ---<br /> <br /> I think at this point it would be better to enumerate different levels and different manifestations of Nephandic infiltration and see how they affect the Technocracy's themes (not to mention our perceptions of the Nephandi).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-10T15:00:12-07:00","id":549407,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Mu_</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/528642-m20-technocracy-human-condition-vs-demonic-influence?p=548860#post548860\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">At several points of the book, it makes reference to the Technocracy as a tool of the Nephandi (I recall mainly the Nephandi and Disparate chapters). It is true that it could be subjective perspective (although no narrator is presented) but also that it is a theme traversing the entire book, too present to ignore.</div> </div> </div> </div>Citations are helpful because they let us actually look at what passages you're talking about.<br /> <br /> The Disparate Alliance mentions it a lot, but as I said earlier, the <i>belief</i> that the Technephandi have thoroughly corrupted the Union, whether it is true or not, is a key part of the DA's motivations. A lot of the Disparates believe the Technephandi have won, and that drives their goals, whether it is true or not.<br /> <br /> The Nephandus chapter calls out that it is entirely possible the Nephandi are perpetuating this belief despite it being a vast overstatement of their power so they can sit back as the Technocracy and Disparates go at each other; sacrificing a few pawns while their enemies kill each other.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> If Nephandi run the Inner Circle or have a great control of the Technocracy, few solutions remain aside from war. </div> </div> </div>Yes. But the Nephandus control of the Inner Circle is part of the Mage metaplot that was leading to the big final showdown at the end of said metaplot. It's supposed to end in a massive showdown<br /> <br /> That's also why it's an option.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I know, but I was trying to make an example in a neutral setting. </div> </div> </div>Ok. But all it does is reinforce my perception of your view of the Nephandi being drastically different from what the books actually say. You have repeatedly go back to the &quot;devil worshiper&quot; thing as if that's all they do.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Again, I was not talking of the URSS in Mage: the Ascension universe. </div> </div> </div>Then what does, &quot;M20 response is that there were some devil worshippers who tainted or bribed the people in charge, just to end the world, or at least to make it a worse place. Those bureacrats still needed to be corruptable, but they followed the evil dudes agenda&quot; mean? How are you not talking about the USSR in the lens of Mage?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> It was a an example of how to explain the reality in worlds with or without forces of absolute evil. Here, the USSR is a metaphor of the Technocracy, and how noble goals can be subverted by the ones carrying them. An example that I find quite precise, despite the Syndicate. I would ask to please reread the example with that in mind. </div> </div> </div>The probably is that the metaphor falls apart because the USSR didn't just collapse because of noble goals subverted by individuals within the government. There were lots of external forces trying to make it work, and make it fail.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I don't know what you understand by &quot;cartoonish evil&quot;. </div> </div> </div>Cartoonish means exaggerated into a caricature (aka a cartoon).<br /> <br /> Something that's cartoonishly evil is something that's evil taken to a ridiculous extreme.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> For me, they are those bad guys who appeared in cartoons, some of them cheesy, some of them very appealing, who wanted to detroy the world or do evil things only because they the bad guys. </div> </div> </div>Mage has set up plenty of motivations for the Nephandi besides &quot;being the bad guys.&quot;<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Anyway, then they are stupid (sorry if I misunderstood you, I confess I am not sure of what do you mean with that). </div> </div> </div><br /> I like chocolate more than vanilla. That makes sense right?<br /> <br /> Is it logical? No. Logic has nothing to do with what flavors I like and don't like.<br /> <br /> The Nephandi have emotional reasons for what they do that make sense. That doesn't mean they're logical.<br /> <br /> So no, the, &quot;they are stupid,&quot; thing is clearly not something we agree on. And that's a problem because you keep asserting the &quot;Nephandi are stupid&quot; as a fact.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> They were invented as pure evil, and then a set of artificial excuses have been assembled to justify their existence and their actions, but they are not consistent. </div> </div> </div>From a literary perspective, the same is basically true for the Technocracy. When Mage 1e came out, all the antagonists were ridiculous monolithic evils that only existed to be bad guys. Since then the games have been trying to retcon in better material for them. It's very hard to not feel like you're perfectly willing to bring all the material that made the Technocracy more than just &quot;Men in Black with HITMarks that come to kill you because nonconformists must die,&quot; but you'll ignore everything that does the same for the Nephandi.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ramnesis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/528642-m20-technocracy-human-condition-vs-demonic-influence?p=549186#post549186\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think it's less excluded middle and more disagreement on what the middle is. The situation you present as middle ground, that the Nephandi have infiltrated enough to whisper dark offers and set up tragic choices, seems to fall firmly on the 'not corrupted enough to matter' side. So it sometimes seems like you are hair splitting, or others are excluding the middle, when in fact you are both just starting from different points.</div> </div> </div> </div>Ok.<br /> <br /> I'm using the points established in the book we're talking about. M20 directly discusses what you're labeling as my middle ground as the middle ground between the two other options it presents.<br /> <br /> It's one of the reasons I keep trying to go back to what the book says. Because the book <i>says</i> there is a middle ground there. I think anyone that wants to come from the point that the middle ground presented in the book isn't valid has a bit more work to do in presenting their feelings on this.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-10T15:43:12-07:00","id":549456,"title":"","content":"Okay. I'm pretty sure I get the dislike for the Technephandi plot notion. IMO, it's tied to dislike for the larger forces of evil behind the World of Darkness, but that's really a secondary factor. I don't think using it straight is inherently bad, but seeing how I wouldn't use the Technephandi out of the box myself, I think I understand.<br /> <br /> I'm still going to argue for it being emphasized in M20. Here's why:<br /> <br /> In previous editions, the Nephandi were never explicitly called out as the most successful major faction of the Ascension War. It was hinted at -- sometimes broadly -- and in some places there were very broad winks. However, it was never stated outright. The Ascension War was Traditions vs. Technocracy, with the other factions occasionally leaping in and being terrifying. This model, conveniently, works for both Trad and Techno campaigns. If a savvy ST <i>wanted</i> to spring a &quot;mua-ha-ha-ha&quot; twist into the war, it was still a twist, but the context was there -- a vital element to all twists. Now, though, the Nephandi have functionally been revealed. It's probably been too obvious for too long anyway, but it's nice to have the acknowledgement. Sure, in-universe it may not be clear, but now that subtext has become text, the Fallen are going to be on a lot of PCs' radar. So how do you keep the Ascension War's traditional bait-and-switch game in play?<br /> <br /> You leave one of the big &quot;Future Fate&quot; options the Technephandi. Make it look like canon. Drop major hints that the Tradtions are in the same boat. Instead of having the Nephandi slithering in the background, make it look like they've gone big. All-in for the triumph of the Absolute. Only the heroes can save the day -- by doing what they've always wanted to do and bringing down &quot;the enemy.&quot; Meanwhile, as ever, the Nephandi sit back and laugh.<br /> <br /> The problem with corruption is that it's usually subtle. Insidious. Obvious corruption falls quickly. Only when monsters think their power is absolute do the masks come off (c.f. Nephandi 1945), and even at that, they're wrong more often than not (ibid). The Nephandi are corruption incarnate. Make their real plans too obvious in the text, and they don't really work. Sure, those of us who've been at this a really long time can guess what the Nephandi are really up to (and make it up as STs, which is very convenient), but to newer players, and in-universe Awakened who lack most of our knowledge, the Nephandi are largely a foul, terrible mystery. While I expect the coming Book of the Fallen to have some major revelations and nightmarishly fascinating possibilities, a certain amount of obscuring (especially in the players' introduction to the universe) is very much in order, IMO.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Heroes die. Hope is a phoenix.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2434-captain-aeon","name":"Captain Aeon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2434_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"203"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-11T01:35:07-07:00","id":549890,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/528642-m20-technocracy-human-condition-vs-demonic-influence?p=549407#post549407\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Citations are helpful because they let us actually look at what passages you're talking about.<br /> <br /> The Disparate Alliance mentions it a lot, but as I said earlier, the <i>belief</i> that the Technephandi have thoroughly corrupted the Union, whether it is true or not, is a key part of the DA's motivations. A lot of the Disparates believe the Technephandi have won, and that drives their goals, whether it is true or not.<br /> <br /> The Nephandus chapter calls out that it is entirely possible the Nephandi are perpetuating this belief despite it being a vast overstatement of their power so they can sit back as the Technocracy and Disparates go at each other; sacrificing a few pawns while their enemies kill each other.</div> </div> </div> </div>Too much work to search the entire book, but I think those two are the main parts. Apart from the trouble of how Disparates come to know or believe the Technephandi corruption, the references to that are constant, enough to say that it is a main theme of the book.<br /> <br /> In the Technocracy chapter there are some references apart from the specific sidebar:<br /> <br /> Page 179: a reference to &quot;those rumors about Nephandic corruption&quot;<br /> Page 185: &quot;It\u2019s a good bet that the pending re-org comes courtesy of the Nephandic Inner Circle&quot;, asuming that there is such NIC, at least if a re-org would be set to purge the idealists (in a nephandic free Technocracy there could be such re-org also, in fact, there was a similar re-org when OoR became the Technocratic Union, but I suppose they were Nephandi then).<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/528642-m20-technocracy-human-condition-vs-demonic-influence?p=549407#post549407\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Then what does, &quot;M20 response is that there were some devil worshippers who tainted or bribed the people in charge, just to end the world, or at least to make it a worse place. Those bureacrats still needed to be corruptable, but they followed the evil dudes agenda&quot; mean? How are you not talking about the USSR in the lens of Mage?</div> </div> </div> </div>Means &quot;applying the same mindset of M20's Nephandic Technocracy to a neutral setting&quot;. It's a metaphore in which USSR is the Technocracy and the devil worshippers are the Nephandi.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/528642-m20-technocracy-human-condition-vs-demonic-influence?p=549407#post549407\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The probably is that the metaphor falls apart because the USSR didn't just collapse because of noble goals subverted by individuals within the government. There were lots of external forces trying to make it work, and make it fail.</div> </div> </div> </div>Just like Technocracy. There were a strong oposition. There were sabotage. There wer a war, a cold war, but a war anyway. But still, the main responsability of the USSR failings (I was not talking about the collapse, but about the repression and lack of freedom) is at the USSR.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/528642-m20-technocracy-human-condition-vs-demonic-influence?p=549407#post549407\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Cartoonish means exaggerated into a caricature (aka a cartoon).<br /> <br /> Something that's cartoonishly evil is something that's evil taken to a ridiculous extreme.</div> </div> </div> </div>But Nephandi are absolutely evil. They want to destroy the world, and meanwhile they make every effort to make it worse. How far can they be from that extreme?<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/528642-m20-technocracy-human-condition-vs-demonic-influence?p=549407#post549407\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Mage has set up plenty of motivations for the Nephandi besides &quot;being the bad guys.&quot;</div> </div> </div> </div>Motivations that are not consistent.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/528642-m20-technocracy-human-condition-vs-demonic-influence?p=549407#post549407\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I like chocolate more than vanilla. That makes sense right?<br /> <br /> Is it logical? No. Logic has nothing to do with what flavors I like and don't like.<br /> <br /> The Nephandi have emotional reasons for what they do that make sense. That doesn't mean they're logical.<br /> <br /> So no, the, &quot;they are stupid,&quot; thing is clearly not something we agree on. And that's a problem because you keep asserting the &quot;Nephandi are stupid&quot; as a fact.</div> </div> </div> </div>But destroying the world and making the world suffer is not an ice-cream flavor. By destroying the world, they are harming themselves. By making it a worse place, probably they are also harming themselves. They are dedicating their lives and energies in those goals, and risking being revealed and suffer death or worse, without winning anything at all.<br /> <br /> When I say that they do not make sense I am saying that the correlation of causes are non sequitur. If someone who was not a moron knew the world is ending, she either:<br /> <br /> 1) Would try to live his life at maximum and get how much pleasure they could before it ends.<br /> <br /> 2) Would try to slow the destruction as much as she could.<br /> <br /> 3) Asuming the world needs an euthanasia, which would be a FORCED euthanasia, killing a lot of people who do sitll want to live their lives, I remember you euthanasia means &quot;good death&quot;. It could make something similar to sense if they pursued to end the world painlessly, but they take pleasure from making everyone suffer (remember the meat grinder example).<br /> <br /> The book presents the sick world as an excuse from them, trying to sell something like idealistic of their position. But then, it jumps to pure evil. What good can do sects like Decadenti, Pipers and Golden Bull in bringing an end to the world. They are plain evil dressed with excuses.<br /> <br /> For me, there is only two explanaiton for the Nephandi:<br /> <br /> 1) They are mad, being something similar to an specific kind of Marauders.<br /> <br /> 2) They are possessed and controlled by the Wyrm or the Oblivion.<br /> <br /> Any of those two poses a credible explanation for them, but they don't make interesting villains to dominate the game setting.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/528642-m20-technocracy-human-condition-vs-demonic-influence?p=549407#post549407\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">From a literary perspective, the same is basically true for the Technocracy. When Mage 1e came out, all the antagonists were ridiculous monolithic evils that only existed to be bad guys. Since then the games have been trying to retcon in better material for them. It's very hard to not feel like you're perfectly willing to bring all the material that made the Technocracy more than just &quot;Men in Black with HITMarks that come to kill you because nonconformists must die,&quot; but you'll ignore everything that does the same for the Nephandi.</div> </div> </div> </div>Even then, the Technocracy was very evil, but they had credible motives, they didn't do what they did just for ending the world or making it a worse place. That allowed that a lot of people, even before the GttT, to see the bright part of the Technocracy. I recently read part of the Book of Chantries, and even being a book from the first year of Mage: the Ascension, even when all the Construction were place of inhuman slavery, there were some decent idealist Technocrat characters. When GttT came out, they had a base they could use to make a Technocracy that, still was evil in many points, but had some lights.<br /> <br /> There is no such base for the Nephandi. They were invented as pure evil and any justification they have been given is artificial. If they were to make the Nephandi less manichean evil, they had to retcon the entire concept of them. They even don't recommend you to play one because &quot;those aspects have power&quot; it could &quot;influence your daily life&quot; (sidebar from page 224).<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/528642-m20-technocracy-human-condition-vs-demonic-influence?p=549407#post549407\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It's one of the reasons I keep trying to go back to what the book says. Because the book <i>says</i> there is a middle ground there. I think anyone that wants to come from the point that the middle ground presented in the book isn't valid has a bit more work to do in presenting their feelings on this.</div> </div> </div> </div>The book's middle points still accept that the evil doings of Technocracy are the deeds of the Nephandi (see also sidebar from page 223).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3673-mu_","name":"Mu_","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3673_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"99"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-11T05:30:02-07:00","id":549976,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/528642-m20-technocracy-human-condition-vs-demonic-influence?p=549407#post549407\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Ok.<br /> <br /> I'm using the points established in the book we're talking about. M20 directly discusses what you're labeling as my middle ground as the middle ground between the two other options it presents.<br /> <br /> It's one of the reasons I keep trying to go back to what the book says. Because the book <i>says</i> there is a middle ground there. I think anyone that wants to come from the point that the middle ground presented in the book isn't valid has a bit more work to do in presenting their feelings on this.</div> </div> </div> </div>If you are referring to the sidebar on page 233 then you are actually just reaffirming my point. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>M20, pg 233, sidebar, second option</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\"> <span style=\"font-size:14px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:FuturaPT-Heavy\"><span style=\"font-family:FuturaPT-Heavy\"><span style=\"font-family:FuturaPT-Heavy\">The Nephandi have the upper hand: </span></span></span><span style=\"font-family:FuturaPT-Book\"><span style=\"font-family:FuturaPT-Book\"><span style=\"font-family:FuturaPT-Book\">They command frightening amounts of influence, poison the Inner Circle, and present the biggest challenge this world might face. Still, there\u2019s hope, and a determined resistance can hunt them </span></span></span></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:FuturaPT-Book\"><span style=\"font-size:10px\"><span style=\"font-family:FuturaPT-Book\"><span style=\"font-size:10px\"><span style=\"font-family:FuturaPT-Book\"><span style=\"font-size:10px\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">down and drive them out.</span><br /> </span></span></span></span></span></span></span></div> </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> Depending on how much influence you think the Nephandi need to have before the Technocracy's themes are damaged, that second option could range from &quot;not corrupted enough to matter, their decisions are still their own&quot; or so close to &quot;complete pawns with no free will&quot; as to make no difference. It all depends on where you start. The middle option is valid, of course its valid, but its not necessarily the middle ground. Nephandic Influence and Nephandic Damage to Themes are different things, and they do not map out one to one.<br /> <br /> ---<br /> <br /> To continue with what I said in my last post, it might be helpful to lay out the kinds of corruption themes that the Technocracy play into and how the Nephandi can help or hinder I think the main three mentioned so far are:<br /> <br /> <b>Systemic Corruption</b> - where the corruption is built into the system via the way it is set up, in the case of the Technocracy because of conflicting goals and lack of transparency. The Nephandi can exacerbate this one, but they don't really play into it at all.<br /> <br /> <b>Noble Cause Corruption</b> - where unethical things are done in the pursuit of a higher cause. The Nephandi can facilitate this one quite a bit by providing the tough choices and the unethical opportunities, but they can also push it to the sidelines if they become so dominant that they've supplanted the original cause.<br /> <br /> <b>The Best System we Have</b> - On a more personal level a Technocrat may know the system is crap, but still think they can do some good from within. Since this one gets very interesting if the Technocrat in question doesn't know how compromised his faction is, the Nephandi play into this one quite well.<br /> <br /> And, to flip it around to the Nephandi's themes for a moment:<br /> <br /> <b>We're not speaking the same language, are we?</b> - Mage is a game about tunnel vision and getting trapped by your own world view. If the Traditions and Technocracy are the two coherent, understandable forces, the Nephandi are supposed to be the coherent yet not understandable one*. Their world view is supposed to be alien and inimical to other mages, and <i>vice versa</i>. The more of them that are entrenched in other factions, the more this theme is pushed to the edges. However, since few people ever see the inner council, and Mages get kind of strange anyways, actual control of the technocracy doesn't hurt this theme much at all.<br /> <br /> *The marauders are the incoherent one.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-11T05:45:04-07:00","id":550027,"title":"","content":"Heavy Arms, the thing is... The Technocracy was designed to be someone who does horrible things for good reasons. They are supposed to be responsible for their own shit. Of course there are Technephandi, there always were. But as a whole the responsibility for what they do was squarely on their shoulders. They are supposed to be glorious transhumanist lords of humanity whose duty is to achieve human Manifest Destiny and they did scary shit and forgot what they are all about.<br /> <br /> Any story about the Technocracy should be a story about coping with these burdens and choosing the right path for the future. Even implications in the disparate and technocracy chapters of the Nephandi being in charge take away their responsibility. We were misled by our superiors. We were tricked by evil Mages. It's a cheap copout that allows them to justify themselves to themselves and others.<br /> <br /> A systemic pattern and control of Nephandi is implied through the Disparate and Technocracy chapters, to the point where it even says that they have enough mojo that they can actually restructure Technocracy as they will. This Nephandic Inner Circle is... bleh.<br /> <br /> I want Nephandi in the Technocracy. I just don't want them to be the patsies for all the wrong things the Technocracy did. And they would be if the main command structure of a hierarchical structure has been giving instructions all along. The Devil Made Me Do It is way too convenient.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:arial\">What doesn't kill you, makes you...<i> stranger</i>.</span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1182-asmodai","name":"Asmodai","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1182_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2989"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-11T07:00:42-07:00","id":550068,"title":"","content":"Especially if the Devil didn't had to actually corrupt me to make me Do It, but limited itself to give me the order. Sure, I could have disobeyed, but that would be treason...","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-11T10:17:58-07:00","id":550312,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Asmodai</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/528642-m20-technocracy-human-condition-vs-demonic-influence?p=550027#post550027\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Heavy Arms, the thing is... The Technocracy was designed to be someone who does horrible things for good reasons. They are supposed to be responsible for their own shit. Of course there are Technephandi, there always were. But as a whole the responsibility for what they do was squarely on their shoulders. They are supposed to be glorious transhumanist lords of humanity whose duty is to achieve human Manifest Destiny and they did scary shit and forgot what they are all about.<br /> <br /> Any story about the Technocracy should be a story about coping with these burdens and choosing the right path for the future. Even implications in the disparate and technocracy chapters of the Nephandi being in charge take away their responsibility. We were misled by our superiors. We were tricked by evil Mages. It's a cheap copout that allows them to justify themselves to themselves and others.<br /> <br /> A systemic pattern and control of Nephandi is implied through the Disparate and Technocracy chapters, to the point where it even says that they have enough mojo that they can actually restructure Technocracy as they will. This Nephandic Inner Circle is... bleh.<br /> <br /> I want Nephandi in the Technocracy. I just don't want them to be the patsies for all the wrong things the Technocracy did. And they would be if the main command structure of a hierarchical structure has been giving instructions all along. The Devil Made Me Do It is way too convenient.</div> </div> </div> </div>Overall, yes. Exactly. <br /> <br /> If you are talking about the original design of the Technocracy, I don't think they there to represent &quot;horrible things for good reasons&quot;. The original Technocracy was pretty much the Technephandi sans Nephandi. They were a monolithic big bad that represented one of the three poles of badness which hemmed in the Tradtions from making the world a better place for golden-hearted weirdo misfits. <br /> <br /> And another broadcast question: Why does Mage need a Big Bad? <br /> <br /> It had one back in 1st Ed, but, with the advent of a less hivemind transhumanist (with all the humanist bits edited out) Technocracy, it seems like the other versions of Mage did without one. Except maybe, to some degree, &quot;we met the the enemy and they are us&quot; and/or &quot;the sub-entropic principle of apathy&quot;. Which aren't really bad guys per se. More existential threats. <br /> <br /> I've run games where the Boss bad guy was a Nephandus with his fingers oh so discretely in oh so many pies. Discrete enough that I had to point it out after the chronicle was over. I've had Marauders play a big part as bad guys, though they are more equivocally so, as being largely random, I haven't focused the game on one. Yet. It would be like running a game where they main threat was an ongoing natural disaster in mostly human form. (Intriguing...). The Technocracy with no detectable hints of Nephandi have been the bad guys. All of the above. Normal humans. Other supernaturals. Other Traditions mages. Usually some messy sloppy combination of all of the above. Overall, though the biggest bad the characters face across the board across almost all the chronicles is themselves<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-11T12:31:07-07:00","id":550548,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Mu_</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/528642-m20-technocracy-human-condition-vs-demonic-influence?p=549890#post549890\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Page 179: a reference to &quot;those rumors about Nephandic corruption&quot;</div> </div> </div> </div>Or more specifically, &quot;And if those rumors about Nephandic corruption are true,...&quot;<br /> <br /> So, completely ambiguous and not treated as fact.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Page 185: &quot;It\u2019s a good bet that the pending re-org comes courtesy of the Nephandic Inner Circle&quot;, asuming that there is such NIC, at least if a re-org would be set to purge the idealists (in a nephandic free Technocracy there could be such re-org also, in fact, there was a similar re-org when OoR became the Technocratic Union, but I suppose they were Nephandi then). </div> </div> </div>A Future Fate sidebar called out as completely optional.<br /> <br /> So, we're still in the same place of the Technephandi victory being completely optional in how the book presents it.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> But Nephandi are absolutely evil. </div> </div> </div>The Nephandi are evil. The books present many of them that have motivations that aren't so absolute. Then there's the &quot;how absolute can anything actually be in a game about reality altering people,&quot; issue.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> They want to destroy the world, and meanwhile they make every effort to make it worse. How far can they be from that extreme? </div> </div> </div>Extreme in this case isn't a reference to their goals, but to their characterizations. Yes, their goals are extreme... but so are those of the Traditions and the Technocracy. They all want to change the world in their own ways.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Motivations that are not consistent. </div> </div> </div>They're not meant to be. The Nephandi aren't a homogenous group hive mind.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> By destroying the world, they are harming themselves. By making it a worse place, probably they are also harming themselves. They are dedicating their lives and energies in those goals, and risking being revealed and suffer death or worse, without winning anything at all. </div> </div> </div>Lots of people opt to harm themselves (or at least risk harm) because the emotional reward of doing so outweighs the cost. Winning, especially in Mage, is a highly personal thing. Nephandi individually define what winning means to them, and pursue that. Which is what most people do. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> For me, there is only two explanaiton for the Nephandi: </div> </div> </div>Then we go right back to the issue I had earlier. You're only willing to see the Nephandi in a very specific light. At a certain point that's on you, not me, and not the books.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Even then, the Technocracy was very evil, but they had credible motives... </div> </div> </div>They were retconned into having credible motives. The Union has always had idealistic pawns, but so have the Nephandi. The more playable Technocaracy was an &quot;artificial&quot; justification added into the game to make the Union not the cartoonish monolith it started as. There's no difference between GttTech and the Book of Madness when it comes to inserting more complex motivations to what were initially very flat antagonists. The Technocracy was just taken to a more playable space on purpose, while the Nephandi were not on purpose.<br /> <br /> [quote] They even don't recommend you to play one because &quot;those aspects have power&quot; it could &quot;influence your daily life&quot; (sidebar from page 224).<br /> <br /> Gee... snip around the context...<br /> <br /> But yes. The book (and all the previous books about them) recommends not playing Nephandi. That's fairly irrelevant. That the Nephandi are evil isn't under contention, and the issues with treating such characters as PCs has nothing to do with the role the play in the game as antagonists.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The book's middle points still accept that the evil doings of Technocracy are the deeds of the Nephandi (see also sidebar from page 223). </div> </div> </div>That's not what that sidebar says at all. The &quot;Nephandi have the Upper Hand.&quot; doesn't say that. It says some of the evil doings of the Technocracy can be pinned on the Nephandi, but fighting them is something that can be done. Which means they don't have completely control and plenty of Technocratic evils are being doing without their influence as well.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ramnesis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/528642-m20-technocracy-human-condition-vs-demonic-influence?p=549976#post549976\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If you are referring to the sidebar on page 233 then you are actually just reaffirming my point.</div> </div> </div> </div>Actually I'm talking most directly about the sidebar on page 170. Though the various sidebars all represents dials that paint a more complex picture depending on which you pick and how you mesh them.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Asmodai</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/528642-m20-technocracy-human-condition-vs-demonic-influence?p=550027#post550027\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Heavy Arms, the thing is... The Technocracy was designed to be someone who does horrible things for good reasons.</div> </div> </div> </div>As noted earlier... no they weren't. They were designed as Evil the Man that duped idealists into their service to do horrible things to keep the Traditions down. The hard men making hard choices was flushed out later because their initial presentation was pretty dumb and we're all better off that got changed.<br /> <br /> But this is actually part of the problem. As noted in M20 the Mage metaplot was always building to the idea that the Nephandi were at some level behind the Union's excesses. They were, at first, <b>not supposed to be responsible for their own shit</b>. That was something the books made to make the Technocracy less dull and more interesting. The problem was that the Nephandi secret corruption metaplot was continued even as the Technocracy as transformed in the books from The Man to rebel against, into Hard Men Making Hard Choices that you're on the wrong side of if you're a RD.<br /> <br /> You're treating things inserted into the Technocracy later in the course of the game as things that were always supposed to be what they are.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Any story about the Technocracy should be a story about coping with these burdens and choosing the right path for the future. Even implications in the disparate and technocracy chapters of the Nephandi being in charge take away their responsibility. We were misled by our superiors. We were tricked by evil Mages. It's a cheap copout that allows them to justify themselves to themselves and others. </div> </div> </div>Again, I reject this assumption that moral complexity is lost when it turns out your superiors were even more evil than you thought.<br /> <br /> If this line of reasoning about &quot;cheap cop outs&quot; is true, than the Technocracy focused game doesn't work Technepandi or not. Technocracy PCs are misled by their superiors even when there's no Technephandi; to the point of explicit magical brainwashing if all the social engineering, and more subtle controls don't keep you in line. There are evil Technocrats tricking good Technocrats into doing things without any Nephandi pushing them into it.<br /> <br /> You're basically saying that if the Technocracy's so evil that the Inner Circle would make Voormas give up because he can't hold a candle to them, but aren't Nephandi, then all the themes and moral complexities of the game are awesome, but the second the Nephandi get involved - even if there's no actual discernable difference for 99% of the Technocracy between their shitty immoral overloards and their shitty immoral overlords that hopped in a Caul - everything just stops working.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> A systemic pattern and control of Nephandi is implied through the Disparate and Technocracy chapters, to the point where it even says that they have enough mojo that they can actually restructure Technocracy as they will. This Nephandic Inner Circle is... bleh. </div> </div> </div>This is directly addressed on page 199. The Disparate Alliance chapter isn't implying that the Nephandi actually have gained that level of control. The chapter is <b>telling you what the Disparate Alliance believes</b>. I'm not sure how this can be any more clear than, &quot;So is it true? Is the Technocracy secretly controlled by the Fallen? As we saw in the previous section, it might be\u2026 or it might not be. The important thing is that the Disparate Alliance believes it is.&quot;<br /> <br /> The Technocracy chapter does not side one what or the other. The level of Nephandi control of the Union is left as a dial for the ST to adjust for their game.<br /> <br /> As for the re-org, yes, it has a paragraph written under the assumption of the Nephandi being in control. That's it.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> And they would be if the main command structure of a hierarchical structure has been giving instructions all along. The Devil Made Me Do It is way too convenient. </div> </div> </div>Then don't? That's why so much of this is in the optional parts of these chapters.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-11T13:09:47-07:00","id":550591,"title":"","content":"I reject the Technocracy pre GtTech. Among those guys John Courage was a rogue because he was focusing on the true enemies, and only rogue Technocrats could be the &quot;good guys&quot;. They were a one dimensional villian that was all about the horrors of technology and faceless government goons wiping out all that is different.That Technocracy is not salvageable, and honestly it's not even all that interesting. I am glad you are choosing to read it in such a neutral manner, but that's not the vibe I'm getting from it, and you've actually made me reread a section that rubbed me wrong from the start only to reaffirm my bad feeling about the presentation there.<br /> <br /> For instance, I do not consider Control (or Voormas) nowhere near as evil as the Nephandi . Their hearts are in the right place, but along the way they missed the point of their fight. That does not mean that they have chosen to do these things with the express purpose of bringing pain, suffering and sorrow upon all around them. The Nephandi are the opposite of that, and if they are in charge they have spent the better part of 500 years actually manipulating others into doing harm towards a nebulous dark plan that has been brewing all along. They have manipulated generations of Technocrats and educated them that Anything is Permisible and No Price is Too High. They have effectively created an army that fights all they stand for in name, while flailing effortlessly against them in practice.<br /> <br /> This Technocracy is not powerful. It is not a union of some of the greatest Magi that ever lived. It's a sad joke that went on for 500 years and only helped the Nephandi further their goals and turned thousands of brilliant Magi into faceless clueless goons. I find the whole concept horrifying and destructive to the Union as a concept, goal and narrative.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:arial\">What doesn't kill you, makes you...<i> stranger</i>.</span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1182-asmodai","name":"Asmodai","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1182_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2989"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-11T13:58:50-07:00","id":550664,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Asmodai</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/528642-m20-technocracy-human-condition-vs-demonic-influence?p=550591#post550591\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I reject the Technocracy pre GtTech.</div> </div> </div> </div>It's kind of hard to have a good faith discussion about the Technocracy in Mage in general, and M20 in specific (which is supposed to have tools for addressing all of the original run of Mage, not just one section of it), when you're going to simply toss out most of two editions of the game regarding one of the major factions.<br /> <br /> And really, just like you &quot;reject&quot; the pre GttTech Technocracy, M20 works if you don't run them that way. It's built to let you play whatever era of Mage you liked; or mix and match things. M20 works if you want to have a specific interpretation of the Technocracy in your games. What it doesn't do is make is so only one set of preferences about the main factions works.<br /> <br /> Among those guys John Courage was a rogue because he was focusing on the true enemies, and only rogue Technocrats could be the &quot;good guys&quot;. They were a one dimensional villian that was<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I am glad you are choosing to read it in such a neutral manner, but that's not the vibe I'm getting from it, and you've actually made me reread a section that rubbed me wrong from the start only to reaffirm my bad feeling about the presentation there. </div> </div> </div>I'm not sure what you mean here.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> For instance, I do not consider Control (or Voormas) nowhere near as evil as the Nephandi . Their hearts are in the right place, but along the way they missed the point of their fight. </div> </div> </div>Control's fairly amoral, as it just serves the will of the Inner Council. However the Inner Council is a dial in the game. It doesn't have a &quot;default&quot; evil setting. It can be turn up to 1e flatness, GttTech internal insanity, or the metaplot NIC. I'm not sure it's worth getting into a philosophic argument in the relative ethical values between, &quot;I'm evil, I know it, but I'm still going ahead it with,&quot; and, &quot;I might be doing evil, but I believe in the cause so it's acceptable,&quot; if they result in the same actions.<br /> <br /> That said... Voormas is pretty damned evil. &quot;I'm going to eliminate life so that nothing can die and everything exists in a permanent form of statis&quot; isn't really any different than killing the whole thing. Voormas' ideal world is empty of anything (besides himself) that can actually appreciate the lack of death; because he'd have killed everything that could. Sure we can draw a line between, &quot;I'm going to destroy the universe,&quot; and, &quot;I&quot;m going to kill everything in the universe that can die, bring about things that can die, or bring about change,&quot; but I'm not sure it's a very meaningful one. Especially since Voormas is willing to kill everything else in the universe just so he doesn't die.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The Nephandi are the opposite of that, and if they are in charge they have spent the better part of 500 years actually manipulating others into doing harm towards a nebulous dark plan that has been brewing all along. They have manipulated generations of Technocrats and educated them that Anything is Permisible and No Price is Too High. They have effectively created an army that fights all they stand for in name, while flailing effortlessly against them in practice.<br /> <br /> This Technocracy is not powerful. It is not a union of some of the greatest Magi that ever lived. It's a sad joke that went on for 500 years and only helped the Nephandi further their goals and turned thousands of brilliant Magi into faceless clueless goons. I find the whole concept horrifying and destructive to the Union as a concept, goal and narrative. </div> </div> </div>It's also just one way to handle them. It's not a forced option in M20. It's not the Technocracy you want (even if it might have been a Technocracy that Mage did have before now). But the book does contain alternatives to this. From the Nephandi being a serious threat within the Union but an evil to be rooted out before it takes full control, to them being a side note where a few agents get corrupted once and awhile.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-11T14:09:47-07:00","id":550678,"title":"","content":"Before GttTech you do not really care about the Technocracy and you do not play the Technocracy. They do not have any saving grace or anything to latch on, and they are there to be goons that muck up the ever heroic Trads. Before GttTech it doesn't really matter what they really are because their narrative role is one of villiany, not even antagonism. So, yes. If we are having a 20th Anniversary book that says everything is playable, then I expect the book to focus on the single best change in late 2nd edition - the one that gave Technocracy its soul and actually made them an appealing and engaging faction.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:arial\">What doesn't kill you, makes you...<i> stranger</i>.</span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1182-asmodai","name":"Asmodai","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1182_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2989"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-11T15:21:31-07:00","id":550797,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/528642-m20-technocracy-human-condition-vs-demonic-influence?p=550548#post550548\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Or more specifically, &quot;And if those rumors about Nephandic corruption are true,...&quot;<br /> <br /> So, completely ambiguous and not treated as fact.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> A Future Fate sidebar called out as completely optional.<br /> <br /> So, we're still in the same place of the Technephandi victory being completely optional in how the book presents it.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, in the sidebar about re-org, they are not presenting the corruption as an option, they are saying that if re-org is made is because the Inner Circle is runned by Nephandi.<br /> <br /> Look, discussing each sidebar an section.What we are telling is that the Nephandic corruption of the Technocracy is one main theme in the book, and is presented in a lot of places. If some guy arrived at Mage in this eidition, the book will probably incline him to opt for the Nephandic Technocracy.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/528642-m20-technocracy-human-condition-vs-demonic-influence?p=550548#post550548\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Extreme in this case isn't a reference to their goals, but to their characterizations. Yes, their goals are extreme... but so are those of the Traditions and the Technocracy. They all want to change the world in their own ways.</div> </div> </div> </div>As I said before, some of those villains were cheesy and some other were quite appealing to tell the truth. In the same way, one Nephandi can be presented as elegant as you want, but he would still be cartoonishly evil because what caracterizes the bad guys in the children cartoons is that they made evil for the sake of evil.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/528642-m20-technocracy-human-condition-vs-demonic-influence?p=550548#post550548\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Lots of people opt to harm themselves (or at least risk harm) because the emotional reward of doing so outweighs the cost. Winning, especially in Mage, is a highly personal thing. Nephandi individually define what winning means to them, and pursue that. Which is what most people do.</div> </div> </div> </div>You'll have to agree that this is a quite stupid way of winning.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/528642-m20-technocracy-human-condition-vs-demonic-influence?p=550548#post550548\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Then we go right back to the issue I had earlier. You're only willing to see the Nephandi in a very specific light. At a certain point that's on you, not me, and not the books.</div> </div> </div> </div>I do not refuse to read the book and trying to understand it. That's different to swallowing each thing I read. I analyze and they are note credible, so I try to find a suitable explanation.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/528642-m20-technocracy-human-condition-vs-demonic-influence?p=550548#post550548\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">They were retconned into having credible motives. The Union has always had idealistic pawns, but so have the Nephandi. The more playable Technocaracy was an &quot;artificial&quot; justification added into the game to make the Union not the cartoonish monolith it started as. There's no difference between GttTech and the Book of Madness when it comes to inserting more complex motivations to what were initially very flat antagonists. The Technocracy was just taken to a more playable space on purpose, while the Nephandi were not on purpose.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's not completely true. As someone who loved science and progress, I could see the merits of Technocracy even before GttT. The Order of Reason rised against the abuses of the wizards. They took the world out of the worst Dark Ages, increased the lifespan and brought the technology we enjoy. I was not the only, I recall reading fan material with positive Technocrats, as well as discussions about who were the true good guys.<br /> <br /> I also always liked the bad guys, but only when they had true motivations. I liked the Sabbat and the Tecnocracy, but not the Wyrm (nor 1st edition Setites). That's why I opt for a flawed or even evil Technocracy, but not one dominated by absolute evil guys.<br /> <br /> As a recomendation from you I'll read the Book of Madness to see the motivations you are referring to.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3673-mu_","name":"Mu_","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3673_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"99"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-11T20:12:33-07:00","id":551020,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Asmodai</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/528642-m20-technocracy-human-condition-vs-demonic-influence?p=550678#post550678\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Before GttTech you do not really care about the Technocracy and you do not play the Technocracy.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's a personal preference. Nothing more.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Before GttTech it doesn't really matter what they really are because their narrative role is one of villiany, not even antagonism. </div> </div> </div>It matters because M20 is supposed to be able to run 1e style games, not just GttTech and later style games.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> So, yes. If we are having a 20th Anniversary book that says everything is playable, then I expect the book to focus on the single best change in late 2nd edition - the one that gave Technocracy its soul and actually made them an appealing and engaging faction. </div> </div> </div>Everything is there in M20 to run the Technocracy you want as far as I understand it. It just has other options in it.<br /> <br /> I don't understand what your actual problem is here. Why are other takes on the Technocracy, esp. ones from other Mage books, a problem in M20?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Mu_</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/528642-m20-technocracy-human-condition-vs-demonic-influence?p=550797#post550797\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Well, in the sidebar about re-org, they are not presenting the corruption as an option, they are saying that if re-org is made is because the Inner Circle is runned by Nephandi.</div> </div> </div> </div>The re-org is completely optional and is stated that the default is that it both hasn't happened, and it is at best a rumor.<br /> <br /> As well, it says that it is a safe bet that the NIC would be behind it. Not that it must be.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> What we are telling is that the Nephandic corruption of the Technocracy is one main theme in the book, and is presented in a lot of places. If some guy arrived at Mage in this eidition, the book will probably incline him to opt for the Nephandic Technocracy. </div> </div> </div>I think someone coming fresh to the game would take the &quot;this is optional&quot; part at face value and treat it as optional<br /> <br /> As well, all arguments a long the lines of, &quot;M20 says this so much it is clearly super important,&quot; has already become meaningless when talking about M20. People take something that comes up four times with mabye 100 words in a 700 page, 500,000 word book importance far exceeding what's there.<br /> <br /> The Nephandic corruption is an important part of the metaplot of Mage. That's not a theme. That's part of the Mage legacy. The them in M20, is that there's lots of ways to approach this; even if the concept of it is important to the setting.<br /> <br /> The problem with how frequent it is, is that it is almost as frequent as how often the book points out that isn't inherently true. If frequency matters, the caveats matter too.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> You'll have to agree that this is a quite stupid way of winning. </div> </div> </div>No, I don't.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I analyze and they are note credible, so I try to find a suitable explanation. </div> </div> </div>As far as I can tell, you're &quot;analysis&quot; is, &quot;Mu_ believes the Nephandi are stupid as an undeniable fact. Since the books never address Mu_'s personal beliefs directly, it never disproves this. Thus they must be stupid.&quot;<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> That's not completely true. </div> </div> </div>Yes. It is. Everything you bring up came up later in the game. Not all of it at GttTech, but not in 1e Core.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-11T20:26:57-07:00","id":551027,"title":"","content":"<b>Book of Shadows</b> (the first-edition Player's Guide) had an entry on the Technocracy which featured a Man in Black tracking down a Cultist of Ecstasy who had been selling drugs, leading to some kids ODing. He was a very sympathetic figure, and he wasn't a rogue.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":421,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","user":"Dataweaver","date":". 06-12-2015, 04:47 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-12T03:51:01-07:00","id":551230,"title":"","content":"I have a question...<br /> <br /> If there are Traditions and Conventions for everything else, why shouldn't there be one that reflects 'selling your soul for power'?<br /> <br /> 'Faustian deals' aren't new. Not every Infernalist is a Nephandi, just like not every Shaman is a Dreamspeaker. Also one doesn't have to be Nephandi to do evil things, any more than someone needs to be Cult of Ecstasy to enjoy a good buzz.<br /> <br /> Are they a scary antagonist? Yeah. Giving Hannibal Lecter the power to reshape reality is scary enough. Give him a support group with similar purpose and power, and it's terrifying.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5818-eviltyger","name":"EvilTyger","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5818_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"314"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-12T04:19:56-07:00","id":551238,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>EvilTyger</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/528642-m20-technocracy-human-condition-vs-demonic-influence?p=551230#post551230\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I have a question...<br /> <br /> If there are Traditions and Conventions for everything else, why shouldn't there be one that reflects 'selling your soul for power'?<br /> <br /> 'Faustian deals' aren't new. Not every Infernalist is a Nephandi, just like not every Shaman is a Dreamspeaker. Also one doesn't have to be Nephandi to do evil things, any more than someone needs to be Cult of Ecstasy to enjoy a good buzz.<br /> <br /> Are they a scary antagonist? Yeah. Giving Hannibal Lecter the power to reshape reality is scary enough. Give him a support group with similar purpose and power, and it's terrifying.</div> </div> </div> </div>You might want to make a separate thread for that question, but a cabal in the service of one entity would be pretty hard to beat. Just watch the current season of penny dreadful.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3953-dwight","name":"Dwight","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3953_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2355"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-12T05:39:55-07:00","id":551281,"title":"","content":"Selling your soul for power is seen as rather counterintuitive to the whole concept of Mage, though. You have to be an utter idiot to take what the demons give you and throw away actual proper Ascension.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:arial\">What doesn't kill you, makes you...<i> stranger</i>.</span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1182-asmodai","name":"Asmodai","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1182_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2989"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-12T06:32:39-07:00","id":551308,"title":"","content":"Or, to put it another way, you're a Mage. You can already reweave the tapestry of reality just by willing it so. Why the hell would you <b>need</b> to sell anything for power? Selling it for knowledge, maybe, but not power itself.<br /> <br /> I can see non-Awakened people selling their soul to a demon for power - a person who hasn't Awakened at least doesn't already have reality warping powers already. This is also why you see a lot of Infernalist Vampires, who sell their souls (or whatever's left of it) for knowledge in Thaumaturgy and some infernal benefits. For Mages, however, the need to deal with demons for power just isn't there. Nephandi exist usually because they have some other reason for wanting to serve dark masters.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-12T07:11:21-07:00","id":551370,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Bluecho</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/528642-m20-technocracy-human-condition-vs-demonic-influence?p=551308#post551308\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Or, to put it another way, you're a Mage. You can already reweave the tapestry of reality just by willing it so. Why the hell would you <b>need</b> to sell anything for power? Selling it for knowledge, maybe, but not power itself.<br /> <br /> I can see non-Awakened people selling their soul to a demon for power - a person who hasn't Awakened at least doesn't already have reality warping powers already. This is also why you see a lot of Infernalist Vampires, who sell their souls (or whatever's left of it) for knowledge in Thaumaturgy and some infernal benefits. For Mages, however, the need to deal with demons for power just isn't there. Nephandi exist usually because they have some other reason for wanting to serve dark masters.</div> </div> </div> </div>Knowledge IS power, remember?<br /> <br /> Greed is pretty universal, and that far off goal of 'Ascension' (that few, if any, Mages actually reach) might be a little closer if you can word the deal <i>just</i> right. The deal might actually be what 'Awakens' the character into being a Mage, Or it could be for Immortality (like Faust), or even just shorter term survival. Maybe a place in the 'new order' (like the Omen), which oddly enough, makes other Mages fearing them into a recruitment tool. (Doing a quick web search, 4 such deals were musicians, 2 in the last Century.) Over a specific person, or group of people. (A rival, or a mentor.) Or even for love.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5818-eviltyger","name":"EvilTyger","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5818_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"314"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-12T08:23:20-07:00","id":551553,"title":"","content":"Given the nature of Mage there are a lot of different ways selling your soul for power could work out. You could be putting yourself under the control of the demon, you could be completely conning the demon, you could be coming to a mutually beneficial arrangement that is nothing but positive for both parties. One of the more interesting ones is the demon could actually be your Avatar, in which case selling your soul is no more damaging than any of the other potential Avatar interactions (which admittedly can be pretty dangerous, but its not stepping into the Cauls level dangerous).<br /> <br /> Unless you are a Widderslainte. Then its probably a metaphor for your inevitable fall.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-12T08:50:13-07:00","id":551603,"title":"","content":"I am not a fanatic of rules in forums, but since the new topic has nothing to do with Technocracy nor with the M20 focus in Nephandi, nor the new themes, I would put it in a new thread.<br /> <br /> Anyway, my short opinion. A mage from almost any Tradition always can summon a demon and made a bargain, or compell him to do what you want. Some Hermetics do it, anyway. One mistake, though, and you are probably damend forever.<br /> <br /> But a Tradition of devil summonners would rapidly fall into becoming Nephandus, supposing the other Traditions don't kill them all before that happens.<br /> <br /> On the other hand, the Tradition of Hannibals you described is pretty much what the Nephandi are.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3673-mu_","name":"Mu_","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3673_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"99"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-12T12:07:35-07:00","id":551951,"title":"","content":"Here's an important question then: what does the Technocracy think of the Cauls?<br /> <br /> For the most part the Technocracy is aware that it is manipulating reality, but it gets a little skittish around certain more mystically inclined subjects like the Avatar. With the party line that the Genius is pretty much a representation of the technocrat's own excellence and talent, what do they think the Cauls do?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-12T17:45:16-07:00","id":552381,"title":"","content":"You know, I've been keeping track of Beast Development for a while now, and I'm quite intrigued with the concept behind that game. It also helps that I'm a sucker for monsters of all kinds. But they've been having some bad feedback from the people and Matt McFarland came out and addressed these issues and went back to the drawing board on some of the content. It's not something I expect to happen with M20, but he said something that I feel applies to this discussion and all the discussions we've been having for the past few days:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Making sure that in-character opinions are clearly reflected as such; all of the World of Darkness games include unreliable narrators, but it\u2019s important (especially for a new game) that the reader understands what the game assumes to be true and what a character <i>within</i> the game assumes to be true </div> </div> </div>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:arial\">What doesn't kill you, makes you...<i> stranger</i>.</span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1182-asmodai","name":"Asmodai","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1182_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2989"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-12T19:02:07-07:00","id":552464,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ramnesis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/528642-m20-technocracy-human-condition-vs-demonic-influence?p=551951#post551951\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Here's an important question then: what does the Technocracy think of the Cauls?<br /> <br /> For the most part the Technocracy is aware that it is manipulating reality, but it gets a little skittish around certain more mystically inclined subjects like the Avatar. With the party line that the Genius is pretty much a representation of the technocrat's own excellence and talent, what do they think the Cauls do?</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, in GttT they told that higher level Technocrats were more aware of supernatural and of the consensual nature of reality. But M20 it's not entirely consistent with that idea.<br /> <br /> Either way, it is important to note that it is posible that when Technocracy concieves the Genius as a manifestation of their inner self they could be closer to the thing that when a Traditionalist sees his Avatar as a spirit, demon or whatever.<br /> <br /> So what Cauls do is to subvert that inner self, and while its means are supernatural, I think it is nothing a Technocrat cannot accept, at least an experienced Technocrat.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3673-mu_","name":"Mu_","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3673_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"99"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-12T22:08:20-07:00","id":552550,"title":"","content":"The Caul is a phenomenon - a traumatic experience that does serious damage to the human psyche. The spatial warping, the n-dimensional geometries, the radiation it puts out... They really screw you up. It affects your mind. Sanchez said it ripped your soul apart, but she's always been a god-botherer. Still, from the few experiments we've got on record .....[REDACTED]....... It's like watching your family tortured, your pets butchered and served to you. Room 101 aint got nothin' on it. The worst part about it, though, is it won't affect you unless you let it. Like those nutbags that manage to breathe vaccuum (by dimensionally displacing themselves into non-euclidean zones), this crap is powerful, but it can't break you alone - you gotta let it in. <br /> <br /> And they make that choice as hard as possible. <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/55-necrotek","name":"necrotek","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar55_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"38"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-20T09:07:54-07:00","id":1105722,"title":"","content":"Well, I'll ignore it. Instead, Im going with the intelligent, reasonable option - human beings fucked up the planet, so human beings have to fix it, and capitalism and statism has failed. The Traditions have the real answer, and Technocracy late stage capitalism is collapsing. Sure, there are other ways to play Ascension, but I'll save YAY THE WYRM WINS for Werewolf, where it makes much more sense and where it's thematically and mechanically supported. Mage 20 was essentially YOU'RE STUPID ASSHOLES for both sides. I'm choosing the side I see as right and telling their story, which is what any GM will do anyway for their table. Im using the Disparates as what they were intended in Revised - sideline options, unless a player comes to me with a better idea.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6358-robert-armsley","name":"Robert Armsley","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6358_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"72"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-20T09:15:24-07:00","id":1105725,"title":"","content":"About the Nephandi - the Nephandi, by defintion, do not have free will. Once you step in the Cauls, that;s it. You become a hand puppet. You can't do something similar to Awakening's well thought out approach to Scelesti (one thing which, however else you feel about Awakening, you have to hand that game.) The Nephandi are absolutely objectively evil avatars of universe destroying Oblivion, according to lore. They simply can not work for PCs. Black Spiral Dancers from Werewolf have more agency. If you somehow devise Nephandi PCs, then you're are in defiance of established Ascension lore.<br /> <br /> Um, no. The Caul is not a &quot;change of opinion.&quot; The Caul is a spiritual transformation that changes the soul of whoever undergoes it into an entity who is suborned to the will of the powers of darkness for all time. This is established in the lore. Nephandi have no free will. I do not allow Nephandi PCs at my table.<br /> <br /> If the game is not about the Traditions vs Union conflict, then frankly, I find it boring. Disparates vs Nephandi is essentially another version of Werewolf, and it's a version of Mage I find boring.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6358-robert-armsley","name":"Robert Armsley","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6358_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"72"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-21T09:57:59-07:00","id":1106110,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Robert Armsley</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/528642-m20-technocracy-human-condition-vs-demonic-influence?p=1105725#post1105725\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">About the Nephandi - the Nephandi, by defintion, do not have free will. Once you step in the Cauls, that;s it. You become a hand puppet. You can't do something similar to Awakening's well thought out approach to Scelesti (one thing which, however else you feel about Awakening, you have to hand that game.) The Nephandi are absolutely objectively evil avatars of universe destroying Oblivion, according to lore. They simply can not work for PCs. Black Spiral Dancers from Werewolf have more agency. If you somehow devise Nephandi PCs, then you're are in defiance of established Ascension lore.<br /> <br /> Um, no. The Caul is not a &quot;change of opinion.&quot; The Caul is a spiritual transformation that changes the soul of whoever undergoes it into an entity who is suborned to the will of the powers of darkness for all time. This is established in the lore. Nephandi have no free will. I do not allow Nephandi PCs at my table.</div> </div> </div> </div>Actually it's is not that firmly established in lore, if at all. The lore about the Nephandi is a self-contradictory kettle that is, as I see it, best read as propaganda of the so-called-'clean' mystics towards those that go outside the socially accepted bounds. <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/858849-my-problem-with-the-nephandi\">I have written about this here</a>. (reply number five)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3254-muad-dib","name":"Muad'Dib","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3254_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2059"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-08-14T16:10:49-07:00","id":1490195,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Yama951</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/528642-m20-technocracy-human-condition-vs-demonic-influence?p=547141#post547141\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Personally, I would make the Craftmasons, who went into hiding before the Lighting Purge of the 1800's as a Disparate, become these egalitarian socialist types. Still believing in making the world a better place for man, they're the most egalitarian of all the factions, Technocracy, Traditions, or Disparate alike. They promoted the worker communes of pre-civil war Spain before they flee to Scandinavia and develop the area into what they consider a near-perfect bastion that still needs to have the kinks worked out. They're against the Syndicate for obvious reasons, and against the NWO for being both totalitarian and giving true socialism a tarnished reputation. They find the Traditions being stuck up elitists who thinks they know what's right for the people. Hence why they decide to join the DA.<br /> <br /> The current Craftmasons are a secret society of both aware Sleepers and Awaken. The nine guilds are reformed into NGOs, trade unions, and community militia. They're an electronic democracy using hidden networks to prevent hackers from finding them. And the current leader and a sort-of foreign affairs officer, the Representative, is elected into the position and is an aware Sleeper.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> I wrote up a leftist faction of defector Technocrats who are explicitly eco-socialist. I can shoot you the google link if you like.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6358-robert-armsley","name":"Robert Armsley","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6358_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"72"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-08-15T16:21:57-07:00","id":1490312,"title":"","content":"<b><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">, please stop resurrecting dead threads.</span></b>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Author of <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Cthulhu-Armageddon-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01KUOM7SI/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982844&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Cthulhu+Armageddon\" target=\"_blank\">Cthulhu Armageddon</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Teenage-Weredeer-Bright-Falls-Mysteries-ebook/dp/B075TTN2FN/ref=sr_1_2?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982717&amp;sr=1-2&amp;keywords=Weredeer#customerReviews\" target=\"_blank\">I was a Teenage Weredeer</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Straight-Outta-Fangton-Comedic-Vampire-ebook/dp/B01KNHICCC/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982774&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Straight+Outta+Fangton\" target=\"_blank\">Straight Outta Fangton</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Lucifers-Star-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01MDM9MCT/ref=mt_kindle?_encoding=UTF8&amp;me=&amp;qid=\" target=\"_blank\">Lucifer's Star</a>, </i>and the <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Rules-Supervillainy-Saga-Book-ebook/dp/B07MB89S33/\" target=\"_blank\">Supervillainy Saga</a>.</i><br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/onyx-path-announcements/206-forum-rules\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Forum Terms of Use</b></a><